\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Pope Names Supporter of Migrants to Lead Archdiocese of Washington\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,086 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The appointment of Robert W. McElroy is a signal of the pope's priorities, two weeks before Donald J. Trump's term begins.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  on Monday named Cardinal Robert W. McElroy, bishop of San Diego, to be the next Roman Catholic archbishop of Washington, moving one of his most vocal allies on immigration to one of the most prominent posts in the American church.\par \par \uc2 The move, announced in the Vatican's daily bulletin, comes at a critical moment two weeks before President-elect Donald J. Trump is inaugurated and signals that \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is establishing his own priorities in the face of the incoming administration. Many powerful American Catholics, including Vice President-elect JD Vance, have aligned themselves with Mr. Trump's efforts against immigration and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cardinal McElroy, 70, is a longtime supporter of the pope's pastoral agenda, and is known for regularly speaking out on the inclusion of migrants, women and L.G.B.T.Q. people in the Catholic church and in the United States.\par \par \uc2 He will succeed Cardinal \uc2 Wilton Gregory\uc2 , 77, the first African-American to be made a cardinal, a member of the church's highest governing body.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference at the Cathedral of St. Matthew the Apostle in Washington on Monday morning, Cardinal McElroy directly addressed Mr. Trump's immigration proposals, stating that plans for a ''wider, indiscriminate, massive deportation across the country'' would be ''incompatible with Catholic doctrine.''\par \par \uc2 While the Catholic church teaches that a country has the right to control its borders, it also centers the ''dignity of every human person,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Cardinal McElroy also spoke briefly in Spanish, directly addressing the archdiocese's Hispanic community, reminding them that the church was the mother of all -- ''todos, todos, todos,'' he repeated -- especially in difficult times.\par \par \uc2 His presence in Washington will stand in contrast to prominent Catholics in Mr. Trump's administration. Mr. Vance, who converted to Catholicism in 2019, advanced a hard-line anti-immigrant agenda on the campaign trail last year. Alongside Mr. Trump, Mr. Vance called for mass deportations, promised to end legal immigration programs and spread baseless rumors that Haitians in Springfield, Ohio, were stealing and eating pets. He represents the traditionalist wing of the church, which has gained strength in Republican circles as it pushes against the rise of secularism.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's nominee for ambassador to the Holy See, Brian Burch, is the longtime president of Catholic Vote, a group that helped to mobilize conservative Catholics to support Mr. Trump for his opposition to immigration, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 As bishop of the San Diego diocese, along the border with Mexico, Cardinal McElroy has a history of standing with immigrants, who represent a significant constituency for the Catholic church both globally and in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 As an undergraduate at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 , he studied with Oscar Handlin, a prominent scholar who shifted public views about the role of immigration in American history.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after Mr. Trump won the 2016 presidential election, Cardinal McElroy, then a new bishop in San Diego appointed by \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , told a Catholic immigration conference that it was ''unthinkable'' to stand by as Mr. Trump promised to deport millions of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 He described Mr. Trump's policy as ''an act of injustice which would stain our national honor,'' and equated it with the United States' internment of Japanese Americans during World War II and its dispossession of Native Americans.\par \par \uc2 He also spoke out when President Trump tried to end the program that protects from deportation about 700,000 immigrants, known as Dreamers, who entered the country as children.\par \par \uc2 As other Catholic bishops sought to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  their pre-eminent issue, Cardinal McElroy often argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only one of several critical priorities of Catholic moral teaching. When conservative bishops targeted President Biden, America's second Catholic president, with a proposal in 2021 to deny politicians communion because of their support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, he pushed back, saying that under such a proposal, the sacrament, ''which seeks to make us one, will become for millions of Catholics a sign of division.''\par \par \uc2 Cardinal McElroy has made a case for the ''radical inclusion'' of women and L.G.B.T.Q. people in church life and leadership. In 2022, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  made him a cardinal, and therefore eligible to vote on the pontiff's successor.\par \par \uc2 Past leaders of the archdiocese of Washington have navigated the inherently political nature of the posting in their own ways. A scholar of American history by training, Cardinal McElroy is a rare prelate who holds a doctorate from \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  in political science, and he has not shied away from contemporary controversies, either in the church or in the nation.\par \par \uc2 ''Our political society has been poisoned by a tribalism that is sapping our energy as a people and endangering our democracy,'' he wrote in America Magazine, a Jesuit publication, in 2023. ''And that poison has entered destructively into the life of the church.''\par \par \uc2 In December, as Mr. Trump promised to crack down on immigration once again, Cardinal McElroy and 11 other bishops from California issued a statement in support of ''our migrant brothers and sisters.''\par \par \uc2 ''We want to assure you that we, and our mother, the Church, stand with you in these days of anxiety,'' they wrote, promising ''to advocate for your dignity and family unity.''\par \par \uc2 The date of Cardinal McElroy's installation has not yet been set, though the diocese of San Diego stated that it would take place in March. A new bishop of San Diego remains to be appointed.\par \par \uc2 Though the archdiocese of Washington is home to about half as many Catholics as the diocese of San Diego, it is one of the most prominent postings in the country. The archdiocese includes the nation's capital, as well as major institutions like the \uc2 Catholic University of America\uc2  and the Basilica of the National Shrine of the Immaculate Conception, the largest Catholic church in North America.\par \par \uc2 Cardinal Gregory moved to Washington in 2019 from Atlanta, where he was archbishop, following a tumultuous period when church leaders in Washington were at the center of America's sexual abuse crisis. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  elevated him to the College of Cardinals in 2020, at a time of widespread calls for racial justice across the country and within the church. Cardinal Gregory plans to retire, but to remain in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Elisabetta Povoledo contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Elisabetta Povoledo contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250107el170003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
