\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Kennedy Tries to Ease Concerns on Capitol Hill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noah Weiland \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 He is expected to meet this week with more than 20 senators, many of them receptive to his case to run the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , President-elect \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's choice for health secretary, encountered little pushback from Republican lawmakers on Tuesday as he sought to smooth over controversy from his record as an anti-vaccine activist who has supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Plodding through some of the more than 20 meetings he had scheduled with senators this week, many of them receptive to his case to run the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2 , Mr. Kennedy told reporters that his sessions had been ''really productive, really good.''\par \par \uc2 Many of the lawmakers Mr. Kennedy met with Tuesday, including Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  of Missouri, Senator Steve Daines of Montana, Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2  of Oklahoma, Senator Tommy Tuberville of Alabama and Senator Roger Marshall of Kansas, quickly put out supportive statements after their meetings.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2  of South Dakota, who is set to lead the Republican majority next year, told CNN that Mr. Kennedy had ''done a good job of answering a lot of the questions about his views on vaccines.'' He added that Mr. Kennedy ''understands the value and benefit that vaccines play and have played through the years preventing disease.''\par \par \uc2 The comments from the Republican lawmakers reflected the fall-in-line dynamic among Republicans that other controversial selections of Mr. Trump's, such as Kash Patel, his choice for F.B.I. director, have found on their trips to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 In some of the most revealing comments about Mr. Kennedy's brief tour of Capitol Hill, Mr. Hawley said in a series of social media posts that the two had discussed a broad, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda that Mr. Kennedy had vowed to implement as health secretary, despite positions he outlined during his presidential campaign that supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''He told me he believes there are far too many abortions in the U.S. and that we cannot be the moral leader of the free world with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates so high,'' Mr. Hawley wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy has mostly declined this week to speak with reporters, who have asked him about whether he supports the use of the polio vaccine. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported last week that a lawyer working with him to vet potential federal health appointees had petitioned the federal government to revoke its approval for the shot.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy, who has expressed views about the polio vaccine at odds with the medical consensus, told reporters on Monday that he was ''all for'' the vaccine, hours after Mr. Trump came to his defense. At a news conference at his Mar-a-Lago estate in Florida, Mr. Trump told reporters that Mr. Kennedy was ''much less radical than you would think.''\par \par \uc2 ''I think he's got a very open mind or I wouldn't have put him there,'' Mr. Trump said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy has voiced a range of conspiratorial ideas about health, medicine and other topics. He has promoted theories suggesting H.I.V. is not the true cause of AIDS, and that 5G networks are used for mass surveillance. He has said that the coronavirus was engineered to spare Ashkenazi Jews and Chinese people.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy's candidacy has generated significant backlash in the public health community, including among vaccine experts who have clashed with him over the safety of inoculations. More than 75 Nobel Prize winners signed a letter urging senators not to confirm him.\par \par \uc2 Protect Our Care, a progressive advocacy group, started an advertising campaign on Monday against Mr. Kennedy, in part targeting Republican senators who may be more skeptical of him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy has also received support from some health experts, including a group of around 800 medical professionals who signed a letter organized by MAHA Action, a nonprofit group supporting Mr. Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kennedy's comments to Mr. Hawley and other Republicans Tuesday suggested that he was working to quash any concerns that he would take more liberal stances on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last month prompted former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to call on Republican senators to reject Mr. Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 During his own presidential campaign, Mr. Kennedy said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be unrestricted until ''the baby is viable outside the womb,'' and that he would ''allow appropriate restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the final months of pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 But many of the actions that Mr. Hawley said Mr. Kennedy had vowed to support were part of Mr. Trump's first-term agenda at H.H.S., which the Biden administration has largely reversed. Among the policies, Mr. Hawley said, were a prohibition on federal family planning funds going to groups that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referrals; the revival of a Reagan-era rule known as the Mexico City policy; and reinstating so-called conscience protections for health providers.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tuberville, the Alabama senator, said that Mr. Kennedy had told him that on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he would support Mr. Trump's position ''100 percent.''\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina, who also met with Mr. Kennedy on Monday, noted that he had discussed ''preventing taxpayer-funded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' with Mr. Kennedy. But Mr. Scott declined to endorse him, a sign that Mr. Kennedy may have to do more to persuade different factions of the Republican conference.\par \par \uc2 ''President Trump won a sweeping victory and should have the latitude to choose the cabinet he feels will best serve the American people,'' Mr. Scott said. ''I look forward to learning more and further considering his nomination during his upcoming confirmation hearing.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241218ekci00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
