\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Bolster Newsrooms and  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 803 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This article is part of Times Opinion's Giving Guide 2024. Read more in a note from Times Opinion's editor, Kathleen Kingsbury.\par \par \uc2 ProPublica\par \par \uc2 The political devolution that led Americans to elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again has many causes, but one of them is the collapse of local news. By reporting on things that people can see and experience in their own communities, small and midsize papers once fostered a common culture and a shared sense of reality, while drawing attention to important stories missed by outlets lacking local knowledge.\par \par \uc2 In the absence of strong local journalism, conspiracy theories and demagogy have flourished. Politico found that in 2016, Trump did especially well in the communities with the lowest number of news subscribers, even when controlling for education and employment. In the most recent election, according to polling by Data for Progress, he dominated among those who said they don't follow political news at all.\par \par \uc2 Trump will return to office at a moment of weakness and instability for the journalism business. With fewer resources, many media institutions will find it harder to stand up to him, especially if and when he targets their owners' other business interests.\par \par \uc2 In this climate, the work of the nonprofit, donor-supported news organization \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  is more important than ever. In 2021 and 2022, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  used an enormous tranche of leaked I.R.S. data to document how billionaires like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  avoid paying their fair share of income taxes. In 2023, it revealed how billionaire donors funded lavish perks for the Supreme Court justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . This year, it's documented how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans led to the deaths of women in Georgia and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Besides taking on major national stories, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  is trying to fill some of the void left by the implosion of local news. Through its Local Reporting Network, it pays the salary of journalists who want to undertake expensive, time-consuming investigations that their home outlets can't afford. In Idaho, for example, a probe into the state's crumbling school buildings, done in concert with The Idaho Statesman, led the state's Republican government to approve $2 billion to fix or replace them. Such work may not drive clicks, but by spurring tangible improvements in people's lives, it may be the best way to start rebuilding trust in the news.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pill Sustainability Fund\par \par \uc2 After Roe v. Wade was overturned, blue states like California, Massachusetts and New York rushed to pass shield laws to protect doctors treating women from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. For some doctors, that means providing abortions to women who travel from out of state, but for others, it means sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to places where terminating a pregnancy has become illegal. That work could get very dangerous when Trump takes over, especially if his Justice Department tries to revive the Comstock Act, a long-dormant 1873 anti-vice law, to prosecute anyone who sends \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication through the mail.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pill Sustainability Fund, a subsidiary of the nonprofit Healthcare Across Borders, offers a way to support doctors risking their safety to offer this essential care, allowing them to reach more women in need.\par \par \uc2 The fund works with a network of doctors who operate on a sliding-scale basis. They charge patients $150, but will accept whatever they can afford, which is sometimes nothing. According to Jodi Jacobson, the longtime \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist who founded the fund, about a fifth of the women served can't pay the full amount. That means that in addition to braving legal jeopardy, these doctors are also taking a financial hit. Jacobson told me that one practice she works with incurred an $87,000 loss in just one month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pill Sustainability Fund partly reimburses doctors for the free or low-cost care they're offering, helping them to stay afloat. ''We will continue doing it as long as they continue to provide,'' Jacobson said. ''And they are telling me right now they're going to provide no matter what.''\par \par \uc2 This article is part of Times Opinion's Giving Guide 2024 . The author has no direct connection to the organizations mentioned. If you are interested in any organization mentioned in Times Opinion's Giving Guide 2024, please go directly to its website. Neither the authors nor The Times will be able to address queries about the groups or facilitate donations.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241208ekc80007u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
