\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 2  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans in Wyoming Are Unconstitutional, a Judge Rules\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 543 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ruling found that two state laws -- one barring use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and one banning all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- violated the state Constitution's ''fundamental right to make health care decisions.''\par \par \uc2 A Wyoming judge ruled on Monday that two state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans -- including the first state law specifically banning the use of pills for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- violated the Wyoming Constitution and could not be enforced.\par \par \uc2 Judge Melissa Owens of Teton County District Court wrote in her ruling that both the ban on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a broader ban against all methods of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''impede the fundamental right to make health care decisions for an entire class of people, pregnant women.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes suspend a woman's right to make her own health care decisions during the entire term of a pregnancy and are not reasonable or necessary to protect the health and general welfare of the people.''\par \par \uc2 Enforcement of the two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, passed last year, had been temporarily halted by Judge Owens while the court case proceeded. Her decision on Monday blocks the laws permanently, although the state is expected to appeal. Efforts to reach the state attorney general's office and the governor's office were unsuccessful on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 The suit to block the bans was filed by a group of plaintiffs that included two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Wyoming; an obstetrician-gynecologist who often treats high-risk pregnancies; an emergency-room nurse; a fund that gives financing to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients; and a woman who said her Jewish faith required access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if a pregnant woman's physical or mental health or life was in danger.\par \par \uc2 An amendment to the Wyoming Constitution, approved by an overwhelming majority of the state's voters in 2012, guarantees adults the right to make their own health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 In court last year, the state, represented by Jay Jerde, a special assistant attorney general for Wyoming, argued that even though doctors and other health providers must be involved in abortions, there were many instances in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not ''health care'' because ''it's not restoring the woman's body from pain, physical disease or sickness.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jerde also argued that the constitutional amendment allowing people to make decisions about their own health care did not apply to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because terminating a pregnancy affected not just the woman making the decision, but the fetus as well.\par \par \uc2 Judge Owens rejected both of those arguments. She wrote: ''The uncontested facts establish that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes fail to accomplish any of the asserted interests by the state. The state did not present any evidence refuting or challenging the extensive medical testimony presented by the plaintiffs.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Giovannina Anthony, an obstetrician-gynecologist and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider who was one of the plaintiffs in the case, said on Monday night that she was ''grateful and relieved that the judge agreed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is health care and that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans violate the rights of pregnant women.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Anthony said she expected the state to appeal. ''This is not the end of the fight in Wyoming, but for now we can continue to provide evidence-based care without fear of a prison sentence.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241121ekbl00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
