\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Why  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Failed To Lure Voters to the Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Leonhardt \par \uc2 2,051 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 We explain why the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't necessarily help Democrats win this election.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, many Democrats argued that the issue would be the key to winning future elections. It was an alluring idea because it suggested that a central progressive policy goal -- protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- doubled as a savvy political strategy.\par \par \uc2 But it hasn't worked out. Instead, Republicans swept this year's elections even as Democrats made the subject central to their campaigns and even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot initiatives passed in seven states. Today, the Democrats' belief in the political potency of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks like wishful thinking.\par \par \uc2 How could this have happened, given that the Republican Party's opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  really is unpopular? In today's newsletter, I'll try to unravel the mystery.\par \par \uc2 Evidence ignored\par \par \uc2 Heading into 2024, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's political sway was genuinely unclear.\par \par \uc2 Most Americans support substantial access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access: In every state that voted on a ballot initiative in 2022 and 2023, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side lost. The uncertainty was whether the issue could also swing the result of general elections by causing voters who had not traditionally supported Democratic candidates to do so.\par \par \uc2 There was some reason to think the answer might be yes: Democrats did surprisingly well in the 2022 midterms, just months after Roe's demise. But there were also reasons to be skeptical.\par \par \uc2 It was hard to find a single election where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed decisive. Although it might have helped flip a few House elections, the Democrats who won hadn't emphasized the issue more than those who had lost. And not a single incumbent Republican governor or senator lost in 2022, despite attempts by Democratic candidates to focus on the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''It is the only thing we're really talking about,'' \uc2 Nan Whaley\uc2 , the Democratic nominee for governor in Ohio, said in her 2022 campaign. ''We think it is the issue.'' Three weeks later, Whaley lost to Gov. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , a Republican who had signed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions into law, by 25 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 The most reasonable conclusion seemed to be that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had played a modest role in the midterms. If anything, it would probably play an even smaller role in 2024. Polls showed that the people who cared most about the issue tended to be highly educated, politically engaged Democrats. That's a very different group from swing voters in presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 Rather than grappling with this conflicting evidence, however, many Democrats engaged in motivated reasoning. Some were scornful of suggestions that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have limited political impact.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a history of political commentators not understanding the intensity the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue has brought to Dem grassroots,'' one party strategist wrote on social media, predicting that it would push ''Dem performance to upper end of what's possible.''\par \par \uc2 This belief shaped the party's 2024 strategy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ''by far the most prevalent topic in 2024 Democratic messaging,'' Politico reported, ''beating out health care, the economy and immigration.'' The Harris campaign's final round of advertisements mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than any other subject, according to the Wesleyan Media Project.\par \par \uc2 The strategy failed. Instead, many voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and other Republicans, including in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiatives on the ballot:\par \par \uc2 Alluring and dangerous\par \par \uc2 There seem to be a few reasons that the Republican Party's unpopular \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position didn't hurt it more.\par \par \uc2 First, the Biden administration's record was out of step with public opinion on other big issues, such as immigration. Second, Trump seemed to moderate his \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, backing away from a national ban and saying he would allow states to decide their own policies. Third, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , her running mate, refused to answer questions about whether they supported any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions -- and most Americans do.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the demise of Roe has not led to a sharp decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, thanks to efforts by advocates to provide pills through the mail. (Even in most states with bans, abortions increased between 2020 and 2023, Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz of The Times reported.)\par \par \uc2 My colleague Amy Schoenfeld Walker recently interviewed women who voted for both Trump and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot initiatives and heard several of these themes. ''I personally think Trump is someone who picks and chooses his battles,'' said June Crozier, a Florida resident. ''And he is saying everyone will have to deal with this from state to state.'' Similarly, Aly Bennett, a St. Louis resident, said that states' ability to set their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies led her to think, ''OK, you can vote for the Republican candidate while still supporting your views on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 All of which helps explain why the 2024 election was not a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But as the Democrats try to figure out their party's future, there is also a broader lesson.\par \par \uc2 The idea that your own policy preferences make for smart political tactics is very attractive. (The writer Matthew Yglesias calls it ''the pundit's fallacy.'') If that's the case, you don't have to make compromises. You run on principle, you highlight your priorities -- and you win.\par \par \uc2 Yet the idea can be self-defeating. If you read public opinion wishfully rather than realistically, you can hurt your own ability to win elections. You can make it easier for your political opponents to enact the policies you abhor.\par \par \uc2 THE LATEST NEWS\par \par \uc2 Trump's Appointments\par \par \uc2 Trump picked \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  to run a new office he called the Department of Government Efficiency. He wants them to cut, restructure and dismantle parts of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Trump chose Pete Hegseth to be defense secretary. Hegseth is a veteran and Fox News host with no government experience who has often defended Trump on TV.\par \par \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , South Dakota's Republican governor, is the nominee to run the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . She sent National Guard troops to police the U.S.-Mexico border last year.\par \par \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  is the nominee to be the U.S. ambassador to Israel. Huckabee, a former Republican governor of Arkansas who twice ran for president, has been a vocal supporter of Israel.\par \par \uc2 John Ratcliffe is expected to lead the C.I.A. He is a former Texas congressman who was director of national intelligence during Trump's first term. Ratcliffe pursued investigations into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump has selected two Republican House members to join his administration, narrowing what is likely to be a slim majority there.\par \par \uc2 More on the Administration\par \par \uc2 Musk has extraordinary influence in the new government. Everywhere Trump goes -- golfing, dinner, interviews -- he's there, too.\par \par \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  wants to fire F.D.A. employees who limited unproven treatments for autism and dangerous stem-cell therapies.\par \par \uc2 Trump's win is fueling a market frenzy. Investors are anticipating tax cuts and deregulation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will host Trump at the White House today.\par \par \uc2 More on Politics\par \par \uc2 Senate Republicans will vote today on a new leader. \uc2 John Thune\uc2  of South Dakota, \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida are in the running.\par \par \uc2 Trump wants the next Senate leader to help him sidestep the confirmation process for judges and members of his administration.\par \par \uc2 Trump's lawyers moved to dismiss his 34 felony convictions in New York. The judge overseeing the case has delayed sentencing indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 ''Women don't speak with one voice'': For some women, Trump's election is a setback. Others see it as progress.\par \par \uc2 Women are stockpiling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills before Trump's term starts, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 International\par \par \uc2 Russian lawmakers voted to ban media, including movies and advertising, that portrays choosing not to have children as attractive.\par \par \uc2 The archbishop of Canterbury, the head of the Church of England and Anglicanism worldwide, resigned after a report said he had failed to properly investigate claims of abuse stretching over decades.\par \par \uc2 Israeli troops have returned to northern Gaza over the past year, as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  repeatedly regroups there. Fighting has pummeled the area.\par \par \uc2 Climate\par \par \uc2 At last year's climate summit, countries promised to move away from coal, oil and natural gas. Research shows they are burning more than ever.\par \par \uc2 In a win for the oil industry, a Dutch court overturned a major ruling that had required \uc2 Shell\uc2  to reduce carbon emissions by 45 percent by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 Jack Teixeira, a 22-year-old Air National Guardsman who shared classified government records online, was sentenced to 15 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Masked men waved Nazi flags outside a theater performance of ''The Diary of Anne Frank'' in Michigan, CNN reports.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Louisiana blocked a state law that requires public school classrooms to display the Ten Commandments. An appeal is likely.\par \par \uc2 Some children's melatonin supplements contain more than six times the amount on the label, an F.D.A. study found.\par \par \uc2 Opinions\par \par \uc2 Raw milk is more likely to cause disease, but the risk is small. That's an example of nuanced public health messaging that can help build trust in officials, Emily Oster writes.\par \par \uc2 Trump believes his victory marks a new era of Republican dominance in U.S. politics. But his coalition is fragile, John Judis and Ruy Teixeira write.\par \par \uc2 Here are columns by Bret Stephens on ''Jew hunts'' and Thomas Friedman on Trump's foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Mystery: A man went missing for months. Investigators say he faked his death and left his family to meet a woman in Uzbekistan.\par \par \uc2 ''Proud hippies'': Two Missouri poll workers, married for 54 years, died in a flash flood on Election Day. Friends remembered the music festivals they hosted on their farm.\par \par \uc2 Skiplagging: A website will help you find cheaper flights by booking a connection you never take.\par \par \uc2 Ask Vanessa: What is the best thing to wear on a first date?\par \par \uc2 The exercise gap: Women have less time to work out than men. Their health pays the price.\par \par \uc2 Ask Well: Most dermatologists agree that a steaming-hot shower can strip oils and moisture from your hair and skin.\par \par \uc2 Theater: Everyone is giving a standing ovation. Do you have to stand?\par \par \uc2 Lives Lived: Frank Auerbach was one of Britain's pre-eminent postwar painters, known for his unyielding work schedule, revisiting the same models and street scenes again and again. He died at 93.\par \par \uc2 SPORTS\par \par \uc2 N.B.A.: The Warriors spoiled Klay Thompson's return to the Bay Area in a 120-117 win over the Mavericks.\par \par \uc2 College football: The playoff committee revealed its latest rankings, which put B.Y.U. into the top four seeds. See the bracket here.\par \par \uc2 M.L.B.: The \uc2 Tampa Bay Rays\uc2  will need a new stadium next season. Hurricane repairs at Tropicana Field won't be finished until 2026.\par \par \uc2 ARTS AND IDEAS\par \par \uc2 Is artificial intelligence a threat to artists? Alexander Reben believes it can be a collaborator, not competition.\par \par \uc2 Reben, an M.I.T.-trained technologist, was the first artist in residence at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , the creators of ChatGPT. His works include a marble sculpture of an image generated by a robot, carved by a robot, and a camera that can print silly labels for whatever it's looking at. He showed The Times some of his A.I.-assisted creations.\par \par \uc2 More on culture\par \par \uc2 Samantha Harvey's ''Orbital'' won the Booker Prize. She almost didn't write it.\par \par \uc2 In ''Conclave,'' a costume designer reimagined how the pope and his cardinals were dressed.\par \par \uc2 Judith Jamison, who died over the weekend, was a goddess of dance -- and, in Alvin Ailey's ''Cry,'' a ''rapturous pillar of strength and sorrow,'' our critic writes.\par \par \uc2 The anchor Chris Wallace is leaving CNN. He joined the network just three years ago, after nearly two decades at Fox News.\par \par \uc2 THE MORNING RECOMMENDS ...\par \par \uc2 Bake new Thanksgiving pies -- including flavors like coconut-caramel, cranberry-citrus and sesame-pumpkin.\par \par \uc2 Dye your hair at home without regretting it.\par \par \uc2 Keep towels mildew-free.\par \par \uc2 GAMES\par \par \uc2 Here is today's Spelling Bee. Yesterday's pangram was windmilled.\par \par \uc2 And here are today's Mini Crossword, Wordle, Sudoku, Connections and Strands.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your morning with The Times. See you tomorrow. --David\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox . Reach our team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241115ekbf0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
