\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Politics of This Election Were Deeply Confusing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jill Filipovic \par \uc2 1,402 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In some ways, the 2024 election results seem incomprehensible. Majorities voted in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in eight of the 10 states where the issue was on the ballot. But Americans also elected the man most responsible for the demise of those same rights. And in several states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  won, so did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How could significant numbers of voters cast their ballots for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also for the man who helped make it possible to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first place? Mr. Trump boasted about overturning Roe v. Wade and being the most pro-life president in American history, while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  pledged to use her presidential power to protect and expand a broad range of reproductive freedoms. Yet, according to the vote tallies released so far, in every state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up for a vote, more voters cast those ballots for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than for Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 The positions of these voters are indeed incoherent. But they're also reflective of the new political coalition Mr. Trump has built, and the kind of men and women who back him.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures passed in Arizona, Colorado, Maryland, Missouri, Montana, Nevada and New York; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights secured well over half of the vote in Florida, although not enough to reach the 60 percent threshold to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's Constitution. (\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures also failed in Nebraska and South Dakota.) Mr. Trump won the three states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights failed and he's on track to win more than half of those where they passed, often by significant margins: In Montana he won by about 20 points, and in Missouri by nearly as much.\par \par \uc2 The first explanation is perhaps the most obvious: Mr. Trump successfully distanced himself from the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  mess he created. His comments about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been all over the place this cycle -- he's bragged about appointing Supreme Court justices who helped overturn Roe and claimed that he wouldn't support a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, despite voicing support for a partial ban in the past. Project 2025, which Mr. Trump has tried to distance himself from despite having close ties to many of its authors, includes reversing Food and Drug Administration approval of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and criminalizing the mailing of the drugs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump seemed to know these plans would be, to put it mildly, vastly unpopular, and there's some evidence voters bought his attempt to play dumb about them: Nearly half of voters who say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most cases, and more than a quarter of pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights voters generally, backed Mr. Trump, according to CNN exit polling.\par \par \uc2 There's something quite Trumpian about the ''I've got mine'' style of politics that might drive someone to vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in her own state while not caring whether the president she picks will strip those rights from women elsewhere. There are women who may want to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for themselves, or men who want those rights for their daughters, but who would vote for Mr. Trump to crack down on immigrants and other groups they find distasteful.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, for example, exit polls suggest that more than 60 percent of women voted to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state's Constitution, while only about 50 percent voted for Ms. Harris. (Among men in the state, exit polls show that about 60 percent voted for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot initiative, and less than 50 percent for Ms. Harris.) Across racial demographic groups, support for the initiative was significantly higher than support for the pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights candidate.\par \par \uc2 The Trump coalition is a heavily male one, and Mr. Trump has repeatedly run on racial and gender grievances. But the men who support Mr. Trump are not only the evangelical conservatives of the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  era, although white evangelicals remain core to his base. Mr. Trump has courted ''Barstool conservatives,'' men who might not be particularly religious or anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but seem to enjoy Mr. Trump's sexism and vulgarity. These men may not oppose gay rights even if they're vaguely homophobic; they may not oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or contraception and they're sexually active (or at least wish to be). They're more libertarian than Christian nationalists. But they're often deeply misogynistic and find Mr. Trump's strongman shtick and his offensive persona appealing.\par \par \uc2 They're also not new in American politics. In the 1960s and '70s, there were large numbers of men who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and contraception access because they were sexual libertines who appreciated having greater sexual access to women without the responsibilities of pregnancy or parenthood. These men may have been pro-choice and even liberal Democrats, but they didn't seem to be great supporters of women's rights and in many cases treated women quite badly -- Hugh Hefner may be the most prominent example.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the G.O.P. was less the party of Playboy than of religious patriarchy. Under Mr. Trump, it expanded to cater to a broader range of men: Those who, like JD Vance, resent childless cat ladies for refusing to adhere to traditional gender roles (or perhaps for not being sexually available to men who think they are entitled to women's affections) and those who, like Mr. Trump, seem to see women primarily as sex objects and who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception because those tools facilitate male sexual freedom. Neither group truly views women as equals. Both are willing to back an overtly sexist man like Mr. Trump. But unlike the traditional men of the religious right, many of the Barstool men will no doubt vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, too. And so will many Trump-supporting women.\par \par \uc2 Many of these voters may also chafe at supporting a woman, even as they ostensibly cast their ballots in favor of women's rights. Since the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision ended the era of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the United States, news outlets have run story after story about the damage that has been done, citing women who have died, nearly died, lost organs or become extremely ill because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Many of these stories are of women with wanted pregnancies gone wrong -- women who are often talked about as someone's wife or daughter or mother. These stories are crucially important to understanding the human cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and they're highly effective in shoring up support for keeping or making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal. They also keep women in the particular narrative frame of would-be mothers or innocent victims.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's pitch to women was that he would be their protector. Ms. Harris's pitch was that she would restore women's freedoms and their independence. It turns out it is quite possible to want to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in order to protect the women you imagine might be your wives or daughters, while remaining suspicious of women seeking actual power -- and that this might be true of female voters as well as male ones.\par \par \uc2 Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is tied up in broader views about women and our role in society, as those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also tend to be more sexist. The freedom to make one's own reproductive choices is a prerequisite for anything resembling gender equality, and without the rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception, we would see far fewer women going to college, delaying childbearing until they are ready and thriving professionally -- and running for office. But a vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is not necessarily a vote for female autonomy or political power, and does not a feminist make. Plenty of voters are willing to check a box if they think that will help keep women safe. But that doesn't mean these voters are enthusiastic about unfettered female freedom -- or even willing to put a woman in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic is a lawyer and author who writes on gender and politics.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241110ekba0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
