\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Ballot Measures Succeed in 7 States, but Break Win Streak\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,150 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Voters in red and blue states supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but the movement broke its winning streak.\par \par \uc2 Voters in seven states approved ballot amendments establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions, continuing a broad repudiation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enacted since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Missouri, which was the first state to officially enact an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after Roe was overturned in 2022, became the first state where voters ended a ban at the ballot box, with 52 percent approving the measure. A similar amendment passed by 57 percent in Montana, another Republican-controlled state. In two battleground states, Nevada and Arizona, measures to enshrine a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  passed, with more than 60 percent of the vote for each.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement also hit its limits, stalling in what had been a winning streak on ballot measures post-Roe, as voters in three Republican-controlled states, Florida, South Dakota and Nebraska, rejected amendments that would have established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even as they hailed their victories, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters warned that the second Trump administration could use federal power to essentially invalidate the new state-level protections.\par \par \uc2 President-elect Donald J. Trump boasted on the campaign trail of having overturned Roe -- he appointed the three justices who helped swing the court majority. And anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have laid plans for a second Trump administration to use existing federal statutes to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and to use the 14th Amendment to establish fetal personhood, which would effectively criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage of pregnancy. A Republican-controlled Congress could look to pass a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights looked at the results and saw victory. ''Supported by the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry and its allies, Harris and Walz campaigned with fanatical fervor on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and the voters rejected them,'' said Carol Tobias, president of National Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats who hoped that support for ballot measures would also lift their candidates to victory were largely disappointed.\par \par \uc2 The groups backing the winning measures, however, said the victories even in states that went solidly for Mr. Trump were a clear signal that most Americans, regardless of party, age or gender, do not want the government restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Missourians can't agree on baseball, barbecue or how to pronounce our state's name, but we came together today in support of this fundamental truth: Everyone must be free to make their own health care decisions,'' Emily Wales, the president of Planned Parenthood Great Plains Votes, said late Tuesday. ''Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians masterminded this ban, sought to silence the people's voice and worked to undermine the mandate of Missourians to restore access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. But we overcame every obstacle they threw in our path to arrive here today.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups had started the year knowing they had an uphill challenge. They had prevailed in all seven ballot measures in the two years after the fall of Roe, the 1973 decision that established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the federal Constitution. It protected a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states as different as Kansas and California.\par \par \uc2 This year, they sponsored \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures in 10 states -- more than in any previous year -- including in five Republican-controlled states.\par \par \uc2 Florida alone posed a particularly tough challenge: While a majority of residents, 57 percent, voted in favor of the ballot amendment, it needed 60 percent to pass -- a higher threshold than any other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure has faced. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups had never won more than 59 percent in any red state, and Florida's Republican governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , spent considerable political influence and state resources to defeat the ballot amendment there.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, which also had a measure trying to overturn a ban that is one of the nation's strictest, sponsors of the amendment were outspent by local and national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, and lost, with 40 percent of the vote. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and other groups that supported similar amendments elsewhere declined to support South Dakota's measure because they said it did not go far enough to protect the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and would allow the Legislature to pass so many restrictions that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would effectively remain inaccessible in the state.\par \par \uc2 Nebraska was the only state with competing ballot measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . An amendment sponsored by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the first trimester of pregnancy won with 55 percent. Those groups had tried and failed to collect enough signatures to sponsor a measure banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting at conception, but sought to block an amendment sponsored by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups that would have established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, the point when the fetus can survive outside the uterus, generally around 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''They launched and spent over $13 million on an aggressive misinformation campaign with one goal: to confuse voters into giving up their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Allie Berry, the campaign manager for the viability measure, which earned 49 percent.\par \par \uc2 One surprisingly large victory was in New York, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists had worried that confusing language might doom the amendment they sponsored. The measure, which passed with 62 percent of the vote, did not include the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ''; instead it broadened bias protections to include a prohibition on discrimination based on ''pregnancy outcomes.''\par \par \uc2 Voters in two other Democratic-controlled states, Colorado and Maryland, also enshrined a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions. The measure in Colorado passed with 61 percent of the vote, and the one in Maryland was approved with 74 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, voters approved the measure to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with 63 percent. But state law requires voters to approve ballot amendments twice, so the measure will face another vote in 2026. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained broadly legal in the state since the Supreme Court overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 The amendments that won in seven states hint at a consensus that has emerged on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Like those that passed Michigan and Ohio in 2022 and 2023, the measures proposed this year generally established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until fetal viability. States can restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after that point, with some exceptions, especially for the health of the pregnant woman. This essentially restores the framework established in Roe.\par \par \uc2 But even in states that now establish a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights made clear they would continue pressing for more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''Life supporters will not sit back and watch as Big \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  works to dismantle all the health and safety protections put in place to protect women and babies,'' said Stephanie Bell, a spokeswoman for Missouri Stands with Women, the coalition that opposed the amendment there. ''We will continue to fight and ultimately be victorious against the forces who see no value in life.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241107ekb700040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
