\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Nebraska Voters Pass Measure Limiting Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A competing ballot amendment, which would have established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, failed to win more votes.\par \par \uc2 A ballot amendment prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond the first three months of pregnancy passed in Nebraska, according to The Associated Press, outpolling a competing measure that would have established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 The state was one of 10 with similar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot amendments this year, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups looked to capitalize on rising popular support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the two years since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But Nebraska was the only state where anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists had sponsored their own measure to try to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. State officials had said that even if both measures won a majority of votes, the amendment that received more would prevail.\par \par \uc2 The measure put forward by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups would have protected a ''fundamental right'' to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability, the point in pregnancy when a fetus can survive outside the uterus, generally around 24 weeks. The state could have restricted or banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, except when needed to protect the life or health of the pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had tried and failed to collect enough signatures for a ballot amendment that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception. Instead, they sponsored the measure that won, which amends the State Constitution to say that ''unborn children shall be protected from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second and third trimester,'' except in cases of rape or incest, or if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ''necessitated by medical emergency.''\par \par \uc2 The sponsors of the winning measure said that the amendment would essentially affirm a law, passed by Nebraska lawmakers last year, that prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks of pregnancy, with exceptions to save the life of the pregnant woman or to prevent serious risk to her physical health, and in cases of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241106ekb60004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
