\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Closing Messages, One Ad After Another\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her allies have concentrated their spending on addressing the economy. For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , one theme dominates: fear.\par \par \uc2 The closing arguments have been delivered by former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in New York's Madison Square Garden and by Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on the Ellipse in Washington, with the White House as her backdrop.\par \par \uc2 But in the final days of the tightly drawn presidential campaign, the last messages to voters in the seven swing states that will decide the election continue to flood televisions, computers and smartphones.\par \par \uc2 From Ms. Harris and her supporters, those messages cover a mix of kitchen-table issues on the economy and taxes, the fate of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dangers posed by a return of Mr. Trump to the White House.\par \par \uc2 For Mr. Trump and his allies, one message dominates: Defeating Ms. Harris is a matter of life and death. Your death, to be specific.\par \par \uc2 ''How will your family survive another four years if you may not be able to survive the night?'' asks one ad from the super PAC Right for America. It received a $7 million run in October.\par \par \uc2 An analysis by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and the tracking firm AdImpact of the largest advertising purchases in the final weeks of the campaign found clear patterns. The five largest purchases from four corners -- the Trump campaign, the Harris campaign and the biggest political action committees supporting each of them -- show distinct tactics.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris and her supporters communicate multiple messages, while Mr. Trump and his supporters mainly stick to variations on the theme of fear. The Harris campaign and attendant PACs tend to use the candidate's voice and speeches, trusting that she has attained a level of trust and likability that can win over the few undecided voters.\par \par \uc2 Ads promoting Mr. Trump's campaign rarely use his voice, relying instead on narrators.\par \par \uc2 And in the final days of the campaign, both candidates will air much more sweeping ads that invoke patriotism -- without mentioning the opposition by name.\par \par \uc2 Here is a breakdown of the campaigns' closing issues and themes, as seen by voters in the key battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 Fear\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign and its allies have relatively modest advertising runs that use former aides and generals who served under Mr. Trump to frame him as ''too big a risk for America'' and ''unhinged, unstable and unchecked.''\par \par \uc2 But the former president and his supporting groups are in a different realm of horror. In advertisement after advertisement, supposedly dangerous illegal immigrants flooding over the border are shown in grainy or tinted images. Frightening mug shots accompany the darkest of warnings: ''Americans will continue to lose their lives,'' intones one ad from Make America Great Again Inc., a $9.2 million buy that ran 4,101 times over 11 days in late October. In an even larger ad blitz, 5,914 airings in just six late-October days for $19.3 million, the same group said that ''innocent victims'' of Ms. Harris's ''border crisis'' were ''bludgeoned, raped, strangled, stabbed, shot and murdered.''\par \par \uc2 ''Kamala created the border crisis,'' the ad asserts. ''She won't fix it.''\par \par \uc2 In the largest Republican ad buy of the final weeks, MAGA Inc. has spent nearly $23 million for 8,560 airings of an ad that tells of ''a little girl'' who ''was raped and buried alive,'' yet ''Kamala allowed convicted sex offenders to live near schools and parks.''\par \par \uc2 Taxes and the economy\par \par \uc2 Polls have consistently shown that voters are mainly concerned with the economy and inflation, issues that have been seen as Ms. Harris's weaknesses and that Democrats have been trying to address. Future Forward, the main super PAC supporting the vice president, has spent by far its largest amount, more than $52 million, to air two ads focused mainly on kitchen-table issues.\par \par \uc2 The largest of these spots, with 33,000 airings, features a man who identifies himself as a lifelong Republican, lamenting the tariff that Mr. Trump has promised to impose on all imported goods -- ''It'll make everything more expensive for regular people all while giving tax breaks to billionaires'' -- before praising Ms. Harris's promise to cut taxes for the middle class. Just behind that, at $25.4 million in the last two weeks of October, is a spot that features a woman, Rebecca F., a one-time Trump voter. She is watching a tablet as Mr. Trump tells his audience at a fund-raiser that they are ''rich as hell'' and will get a tax cut.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to give tax breaks to billionaires,'' she says in 28,536 airings, ''but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has plans to help us. She's going to crack down on price gouging and cut taxes for working people like me.''\par \par \uc2 Right for America is running one positive economic ad, in English and Spanish, extolling Mr. Trump for his promise to eliminate taxes on tips, Social Security and overtime.\par \par \uc2 ''The people who work overtime are among the hardest working citizens in our country, and for too long, no one in Washington has been looking out for them,'' Mr. Trump intones in a rare use of his voice in the ads. At around $7 million, it is toward the bottom of the largest late buys.\par \par \uc2 Another ad, with about $7.7 million behind it for the closing weeks, hits Ms. Harris hard on inflation. Financed by Restoration PAC, the advertisement appears to be aimed at Black voters. ''They are killing us without killing us,'' a Black woman near tears asserts.\par \par \uc2 Abortion\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade in 2022, voters have been rewarding Democrats, in midterm and special elections, for standing by the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Harris campaign is pressing that advantage. Its second and third largest closing ad buys -- $17.5 million and more than 47,600 airings in the last two weeks of October -- are on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The largest airing, showed nearly 32,000 times, is the return of Hadley Duvall, the young woman who helped Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky win re-election last year with her wrenching story of being sexually abused and impregnated at age 12. This time, Ms. Duvall speaks of the ''64,000 pregnancies from rape'' in states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the repeal of Roe. ''Trump did this,'' she says.\par \par \uc2 The other features Mr. Trump boasting about his role in repealing the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- he appointed three of the conservative Supreme Court justices who helped make up the majority -- with a series of women warning of restrictions to come if he is returned to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Anti-trans attacks\par \par \uc2 Among the Trump campaign's top four ad buys in October have been attacks on Ms. Harris's past stances on rights for transgender people.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have seized on a response by Ms. Harris to a question about medical care for incarcerated people on a 2019 \uc2 American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  candidate questionnaire, as well as comments by Ms. Harris that she ''pushed'' the state corrections department ''to provide gender-transition surgery to state inmates'' while serving as California attorney general from 2011 to 2017. (Trump appointees at the federal Bureau of Prisons also provided gender-confirming treatments, including hormone therapy, for a small group of inmates who requested it during Mr. Trump's four years in office.)\par \par \uc2 One Trump campaign ad refers to a convicted murderer sentenced to life in prison in California. ''\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  pushed to use tax dollars to pay for his sex change,'' says the ad, which ran 17,375 times in the last three weeks of October at a cost of more than $10 million. The voiceover concludes, ''Kamala's agenda is they/them, not you.''\par \par \uc2 A second Trump campaign ad uses similar ''they/them'' language to hit the vice president on supporting ''transgender sex changes in jail'' and ''letting biological men compete against our girls in their sport.''\par \par \uc2 The ad, with 13,445 airings in the last two weeks of October, appears to be aimed at Black men, as it uses a clip of the popular radio and podcast host Charlamagne Tha God raising the issue on his show.\par \par \uc2 Patriotism, in the very last days\par \par \uc2 The final, sweeping advertisements from both campaigns won't show up in the largest buys or biggest runs because they will air for only a few days, but they have two traits in common: an appeal to patriotism and a conscious decision not to name the opposing candidate.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris's two-minute-long finale, which was set to air during N.F.L. games on Sunday, leans heavily on her own voice, ending with her speech on the Ellipse. It opens with a personal touch -- her own -- as she speaks with individual voters and their children, and then she addresses the camera about a new generation of leadership and a baton passed.\par \par \uc2 From her speeches, she is heard saying, ''We see in our fellow Americans neighbors, not enemies,'' as a tableau of the nation streams by, urban, rural, Black and white. The message mixes a lofty appeal for unity and a promise to address kitchen-table issues of grocery prices and prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's one-minute spot starts with a male voice -- not his own. ''Four years ago, we took a wrong turn, and lost our purpose,'' the narrator says. As it paints a picture of a diminished America under the Biden-Harris administration, it suggests that anyone who ''dared to speak the truth'' has been accused of ''hate speech.''\par \par \uc2 The visuals -- migrants, criminals on the attack and the Algerian Olympic female boxer whom Mr. Trump has falsely called a man -- are both menacing and familiar to Mr. Trump's ads. (''Men can beat up women and win medals,'' the narrator asserts, in an echo of the attack lines Mr. Trump and his party have used around the issue of transgender athletes.) But the ad segues to a defiant form of optimism, using the imagery of the assassination attempt on Mr. Trump to embody the pending American comeback.\par \par \uc2 ''When we get knocked down, we don't stay down: We get up again,'' the narrator exhorts, using Mr. Trump's own words from that July day in Butler, Pa. ''We fight, we fight, we fight.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241105ekb50003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
