\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Tightest Contest In Decades Grows Tighter At Finish\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Ruth Igielnik \par \uc2 2,050 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Donald J. Trump has improved his standing in Pennsylvania even as late-deciding voters appear to be breaking for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The presidential race appears to be hurtling toward a photo finish, with the final set of polls by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College finding Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  showing new strength in North Carolina and Georgia as former President Donald J. Trump erases her lead in Pennsylvania and maintains his advantage in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 It has been decades since the polls have shown the nation facing a presidential race that is so close across so many states in both the Sun Belt and the Rust Belt. The tightly contested landscape means the race remains highly uncertain as the campaign enters its final hours.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris is now narrowly ahead in Nevada, North Carolina and Wisconsin, the polls show, while Mr. Trump leads in Arizona. The polls show them locked in close races in Michigan, Georgia and Pennsylvania. But the results in all seven states are within the margin of sampling error, meaning neither candidate has a definitive lead in any of them.\par \par \uc2 [While the overall poll result is largely unchanged since previous Times/Siena polls, there were some notable shifts, Nate Cohn writes. ]\par \par \uc2 Both candidates have multiple pathways available to capture the 270 Electoral College votes required to claim victory, assuming that polls are not dramatically underestimating the support for one or the other. In such a close race, even a small systemic polling error could tip the contest decisively in either direction.\par \par \uc2 But there are signs that late deciders are breaking for Ms. Harris: Among the 8 percent of voters who said they had only recently decided on their vote, she wins the group by 55 percent to 44 percent. (With Election Day nearing, 11 percent of voters remained undecided or persuadable, down from 16 percent about a month ago.)\par \par \uc2 The polling comes as more than 70 million Americans have already voted, according to the University of Florida Election Lab.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 40 percent of those surveyed by the Times/Siena poll across the seven states said they had voted. Ms. Harris wins those voters by a margin of eight percentage points, the polls found. Mr. Trump has an edge among voters who say they are highly likely to vote but have not yet cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 [Follow the latest polls and see updated polling averages of the Harris vs. Trump matchup .]\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has been gaining ground in Pennsylvania, where Ms. Harris had a four-percentage-point edge in all previous New York Times/Siena College polls in the state since she entered the race. The race is now tied, indicating an increasingly competitive contest in the state, which strategists in both campaigns believe could tip the election.\par \par \uc2 William Renfro, an electrical utility student from Las Vegas working as a part-time barista, said he considered himself a libertarian. ''Fiscally, I'm more conservative,'' he said. ''Socially, I'm more liberal.''\par \par \uc2 He said that he had decided to support Ms. Harris because he was troubled by the way Mr. Trump had pushed falsehoods about Haitian migrants in Ohio eating dogs and cats, made inflammatory statements, and appeared with Laura Loomer, a far-right activist. ''But when you have, you know, with that Laura Loomer thing, and then stuff about the Haitian migrants in Ohio,'' he said, ''it's very 'Why do I want this person to lead the country that I live in?'''\par \par \uc2 Rates of early voting are particularly high in North Carolina, where more than half the voters said they had already cast a ballot. Ms. Harris wins early voters in the state by 8 percentage points, perhaps contributing to her three-percentage-point edge in the survey of the state. Despite recent devastation there from Hurricane Helene, more than nine out of 10 North Carolina voters said that the storm and its aftermath have had no impact at all on their ability to vote.\par \par \uc2 The only state where the poll found Mr. Trump winning with people who said they had already voted was Arizona. Forty-six percent of voters there said they had already voted, and Mr. Trump wins that group, 50 percent to 46 percent.\par \par \uc2 The polls also reveal a shift in the issues being prioritized by voters in the final stretch of the race. The economy still remains their top concern, but in states like Wisconsin, where Ms. Harris has held a consistent edge, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now nearly matches the economy as voters' most important issue. And in Arizona, where Mr. Trump leads, immigration also continues to rise as a crucial issue driving voters' choices.\par \par \uc2 Dakota Parrish, 31, who works at an insurance company, said he was supporting Mr. Trump because he believes the country was more economically prosperous when he was president.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not so much about, like, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a person in this case,'' said Mr. Parrish, who lives in Surprise, Ariz. ''People could afford more, could live a happier life under the policies that were put in place.''\par \par \uc2 The survey shows that Mr. Trump has continued to hold on to the core of the coalition that supported him in his past two presidential bids -- white voters who did not attend college, and men -- while expanding his support among younger, nonwhite and newer voters. He is exceeding his 2020 vote share in Arizona and Michigan, both states he did not win four years ago.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris is underperforming relative to President Biden's performance in 2020 with younger voters, Black voters, particularly Black women, and Latino voters. But she has improved on his numbers with these groups since he dropped out of the race in July.\par \par \uc2 The gender gap remains wide across all seven states, with Ms. Harris the favorite of women and Mr. Trump preferred by men. For women and younger voters, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now surpasses the economy as the most important issue driving votes.\par \par \uc2 The battle for the Senate\par \par \uc2 Along with the presidential campaign, the race for control of the Senate has also tightened. In all three Northern ''blue wall'' states, the Democratic advantage is notably diminished in the final days of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Senator Bob Casey, a Democrat, leads by five percentage points, down from nine in September. In Wisconsin, Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , the Democrat, leads by four percentage points, down from eight in September.\par \par \uc2 And in the contest for Michigan's open seat, Representative \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , a Democrat, and her Republican opponent, former Representative Mike Rogers, are neck and neck.\par \par \uc2 Democratic candidates have a stronger hold on the Sun Belt contests, with Representative \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  leading Kari Lake in Arizona by five percentage points and Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  with a nine-point lead over her Republican challenger, Sam Brown, in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 All the Democratic candidates for Senate are outperforming Ms. Harris, an indication that a potentially crucial slice of voters may split their tickets by supporting both Mr. Trump and the Democratic candidate for Senate in their state.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Micola von F\u252\'00\'FCrstenrecht, a Republican from Phoenix, said she had voted for both Mr. Trump and Mr. Gallego because she dislikes Ms. Lake, the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 ''The woman is unhinged. \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  served our country as a United States Marine and I come from a military family,'' Mrs. Micola von F\u252\'00\'FCrstenrecht, 60, said. ''He may not do everything right either, but I would trust him with my life before her. She doesn't give a damn.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats currently hold a 51-seat Senate majority, but Republicans are set to pick up a seat in West Virginia after the retirement of Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2 , an independent who caucuses with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 For the Democrats to retain control of the Senate, they must defend their seats in all five races, while also staving off Republican challengers in Ohio and in Montana, where Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 , the incumbent Democrat, is trailing, according to \uc2 New York Times\uc2  polling averages of the race. Recent polling shows Democrats may have an opportunity to defeat Senator Ted Cruz, the Texas Republican, though their candidate, Representative \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 , remains a long shot in the race.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2 , the Democratic candidate for governor, holds a substantial 17-percentage-point lead over his opponent, Mark Robinson, the Republican lieutenant governor with a history of offensive remarks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot\par \par \uc2 In Arizona and Nevada, a majority of both men and women also back amendments to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into their state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 Such a measure has notably more support in Nevada, where 64 percent of voters say they back an amendment to their state constitution. In Arizona, 55 percent of voters support a similar amendment.\par \par \uc2 Voters have sided with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in all seven states where the question has appeared on the ballot since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the decision that had established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While voters have backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in more conservative states, including Kansas and Kentucky, support for the measures has never exceeded 60 percent in any of those states.\par \par \uc2 Linda Guggia, 46, a real estate agent in Henderson, Nev., said she backed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment in her state. But she also voted for Mr. Trump, who she believes will be stronger on economic issues.\par \par \uc2 ''As of right now, that's not a main focal point in my family. I have a son, you know?'' she said, referring to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''So it's not important, but I understand, you know, the rights of other women and I do respect that.''\par \par \uc2 Camille Baker, Christine Zhang and Elena Shao contributed to this report from New York.\par \par \uc2 How These Polls Were Conducted\par \par \uc2 Here are the key things to know about these polls from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College:\par \par \uc2 Interviewers spoke with 7,879 likely voters across the seven battleground states, including 1,025 voters in Arizona, 1,004 voters in Georgia, 998 voters in Michigan, 1,010 voters in Nevada, 1,010 voters in North Carolina, 1,527 voters in Pennsylvania and 1,305 voters in Wisconsin from Oct. 24 to Nov. 2.\par \par \uc2 Times/Siena polls are conducted by telephone, using live interviewers, in both English and Spanish. Overall, more than 98 percent of respondents were contacted on a cellphone for these polls. You can see the exact questions that were asked and the order in which they were asked here.\par \par \uc2 Voters are selected for the survey from a list of registered voters. The list contains information on the demographic characteristics of every registered voter, allowing us to make sure we reach the right number of voters of each party, race and region. For these polls, interviewers placed about 850,000 calls to more than 320,000 voters.\par \par \uc2 To further ensure that the results reflect the entire voting population, not just those willing to take a poll, we give more weight to respondents from demographic groups that are underrepresented among survey respondents, like people without a college degree. You can see more information about the characteristics of respondents and the weighted sample at the bottom of the results and methodology page, under ''Composition of the Sample.''\par \par \uc2 The margin of sampling error among likely voters is plus or minus 1.3 percentage points across the seven battleground states and about plus or minus 3.5 percentage points in each state poll. In theory, this means that the results should reflect the views of the overall population most of the time, though many other challenges create additional sources of error. When the difference between two values is computed -- such as a candidate's lead in a race -- the margin of error is twice as large.\par \par \uc2 The Times/Siena polls of Pennsylvania in 2024 were conducted in partnership with the Philadelphia Inquirer and were funded in part by a grant from the Lenfest Institute for Journalism. The poll was designed and conducted independently from the institute.\par \par \uc2 You can see full results and a detailed methodology here. If you want to read more about how and why we conduct our polls, you can see answers to frequently asked questions and submit your own questions here.\par \par \uc2 Camille Baker, Christine Zhang and Elena Shao contributed to this report from New York.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241104ekb40003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
