\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Republicans Shift  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Message, Sounding More Like Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison McCann and Vivian Li \par \uc2 1,110 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 x\par \par \uc2 Josh Riley, the Democratic challenger running for Congress in New York's 19th District, has a clear message on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : ''I believe that women's health care decisions are women's health care decisions and that politicians should stay the hell out of it.''\par \par \uc2 And his Republican opponent, the incumbent Representative Marc Molinaro, is saying nearly the same thing: ''I believe health care decisions should be between a woman and her doctor, not Washington.''\par \par \uc2 Across the country's most competitive House races, Republicans have spent months trying to redefine themselves on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , going so far as to borrow language that would not feel out of place at a rally of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Many Republicans who until recently backed federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are now saying the issue should be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 At least a half-dozen Republican candidates have put out direct-to-camera ads declaring their opposition to a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Instead, they say, they support exceptions to existing state laws and back protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, such as I.V.F.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have raised the possibility of a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban should Republicans win in November, and they are framing the campaign as another referendum on the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision, which overturned Roe v. Wade. They are hoping to continue their run of electoral successes since the 2022 decision to win back control of the House.\par \par \uc2 Any new federal legislation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to pass both the House and the Senate and be signed by the president to become law. But whichever party emerges with a majority in the House will have the ability to dictate the legislative agenda, including whether measures to restrict or expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access have the chance to pass.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in California and New York in particular, who are running in swing districts in blue states that favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, have felt the most pressure to address the issue directly. ''If we don't talk about the issue, we become whatever the Democrats say we are,'' said Will Reinert, the press secretary for the National Republican Congressional Committee.\par \par \uc2 To better understand how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is playing a role in these campaigns, The New York Times surveyed candidates from both parties in the most competitive House races about their support for federal limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Times also looked at voting records, issues listed on campaign websites, debate and media coverage, and endorsements from major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups.\par \par \uc2 The Times survey showed that while Republicans are notably focused on what they will not do on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the federal level, their Democratic opponents are talking about what they will do to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Nearly all the Democratic candidates said they supported restoring the protections of Roe v. Wade, which would allow access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, or around 24 weeks, in every state.\par \par \uc2 In attack ads, Democrats are pointing to their opponents' voting records or past statements as evidence of extremism -- despite what they may be saying now.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups said Republicans are misleading voters by claiming they do not support an outright \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''ban,'' when they might support a federal ''limit'' or ''standard,'' such as the 15-week proposal put forward by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 ''They are playing around with the semantics; they are clearly testing out different framing and messaging in an attempt to try and deceive voters because they realize how politically unpopular their policy stances are,'' said \uc2 Jessica Arons\uc2 , a director of policy and government affairs at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republicans in the Times survey almost universally declined to answer questions about gestational limits. Only one, Representative \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  of Nebraska, said he supported a specific federal limit, in the third trimester.\par \par \uc2 The Republican shift away from publicly supporting a federal ban follows the lead of former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who has changed his own language on the issue after seeing the electoral backlash to the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 As recently as 2021, a majority of House Republicans -- including seven incumbents in this year's tossup races -- co-sponsored the Life at Conception Act, a bill that would have amounted to a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. This year, Representative Scott Perry of Pennsylvania's 10th District was the only incumbent in a competitive race to stay on as a co-sponsor.\par \par \uc2 Two Republican incumbents who now say they oppose a national ban -- Representatives \uc2 Ken Calvert\uc2  and David Valadao in California -- voted in favor of a 20-week ban that passed the House in 2017. Representative \uc2 Mariannette Miller-Meeks\uc2 , an Iowa Republican, co-sponsored a 15-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. She did not respond to questions about whether she still supports it.\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans described themselves as personally ''pro-life'' but said they accepted the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in place in their states. Rob Bresnahan Jr., a challenger in Pennsylvania's 8th District, said he supported the state's current law, which allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, when they were not attacking Republicans, leaned into language about personal freedom, with many in the survey saying the government should not be involved in medical decisions.\par \par \uc2 Another common refrain was that the decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be ''between a woman and her doctor.'' Two Democrats used similar language rather than explicitly calling for federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 By appearing to moderate their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , candidates have risked losing the backing of prominent advocacy groups. Only three Republicans in the tossup races received an endorsement from Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, and seven received one from National Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 Two major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, by contrast, endorsed nearly all the Democratic candidates. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  -- whose political action fund is pouring $40 million into the campaign -- endorsed all but six candidates, while \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2  endorsed all but four.\par \par \uc2 Representative Jared Golden, the Democratic incumbent in Maine's 2nd Congressional District -- an area Mr. Trump won by six points in 2020 -- did not get \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's endorsement this year. He said the reason was his vote for the 2024 defense policy bill, which included an amendment blocking reimbursement for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel costs for service members.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Golden said he was not concerned about the lack of support from the group, pointing instead to his co-sponsorship of the Women's Health Protection Act, a bill to restore the protections of Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm quite confident that voters in Maine know where I stand,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Endorsements as of Oct. 30. Note: *Incumbent.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241103ekb30002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
