\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Activist Creates a Platform for Personal Stories\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jessica Testa \par \uc2 1,176 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last week, an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account with fewer than 3,500 followers published a video of a Florida woman named Deborah Dorbert. She described carrying a baby diagnosed with Potter syndrome, a fatal condition, to full term after being denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her son lived for 94 minutes, she said in the video.\par \par \uc2 The next morning, the clip debuted to hundreds of thousands of viewers on MSNBC's popular weekday show ''Morning Joe.''\par \par \uc2 Few videos have their reach jump by an order of that magnitude -- fewer still on a charged topic like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But this wasn't any \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account. It was a creation of Cecile Richards.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Richards, the former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  -- and perhaps the country's most famous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activist -- is a co-founder of a new project called \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America.\par \par \uc2 It is an attempt, mostly through accounts on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and TikTok, like the one that published Ms. Dorbert's video, to bring personal stories of state bans and restrictions to broad audiences. It also represents a fight for attention in a chaotic election season, in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has moved up and down the ranks of voter concerns.\par \par \uc2 The problem Ms. Richards and her co-founders, Lauren Peterson and Kaitlyn Joshua, set out to solve is this: Journalists are writing about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , widely and deeply, but the work does not always resonate, or ''stay alive more than a day or a week,'' Ms. Richards said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Local news media has been weakened, feminist digital media has all but disappeared and the news cycle has accelerated to a hectic, almost indigestible degree.\par \par \uc2 ''The only thing people respond to and remember are stories,'' Ms. Richards said, citing advice from her mother, Ann Richards, a former Texas governor. But those stories have to be easy to access -- namely, short and free and in your face.\par \par \uc2 ''We have to figure out: How do you catch the attention of people that, even if they could find the article, don't have 20 minutes to read it?'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Activists of all stripes have long relied on the power of visual storytelling to sway audiences, particularly on social media and during elections. But while stories about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''are breaking through to many -- and appear to be affecting opinion,'' according to a June report from the nonpartisan research firm PerryUndem, they are not reaching Republicans or anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters at the same level as people who already support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America solution: recording, producing and releasing content on social media. Over the last three days, a video posted to TikTok featuring a Texas man detailing his wife's harrowing miscarriage has been viewed more than one million times. The video was filmed at a launch event for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America, co-hosted by Glamour magazine at the \uc2 Ford Foundation\uc2  in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 The group is a nonprofit project of the Hopewell Fund, which sponsors Democratic causes and organizations. The founders said they had raised about $1 million from about half a dozen donors, including Phoebe Gates, a daughter of the billionaire \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  founder \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the videos and website, the founders are blurring the lines between their advocacy work and traditional news media. Their site places its own footage alongside articles from national and local publications, some republished in full with the outlets' permission.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Peterson referred to this blend as a ''complimentary relationship.''\par \par \uc2 The small group would never be able to achieve the kind of journalism produced by major publications, Ms. Peterson said. But ''there are also people who we're able to speak with who wouldn't trust a journalist,'' she added. The activists did not require women to use their full names or provide medical records to verify their stories, as many journalists would.\par \par \uc2 Still, Ms. Richards said her motivation was to document history in real time, not unlike the goal of a journalist. She pointed to how stories about enslaved people and those emancipated from slavery had not been told until decades later.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't want to come back 30 years later,'' she said. ''Nor could I.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Richards, 67, was diagnosed in 2023 with a glioblastoma, a type of brain tumor. She revealed the diagnosis in a January New York magazine article that described the median survival rate for the condition as 15 months.\par \par \uc2 ''I was doing really pretty well for the last year,'' said Ms. Richards, who spoke at the Democratic National Convention in August. Then the cancer began affecting her balance. She now uses a walker, and her speech is slightly slower.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Richards is continuing to try new medications and treatments. ''And who knows?'' she said during an interview at her apartment on Central Park. She works on the storytelling project as much as her treatment schedule allows.\par \par \uc2 But often a story has to be told multiple times in multiple ways to resonate.\par \par \uc2 To Jessica Valenti, a longtime \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist and a writer who is not affiliated with \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America, repetition is essential.\par \par \uc2 ''You have to assume every day that someone hasn't seen it and just continually repeat it,'' she said. ''There is this sense of overwhelm with readers, where these stories are horrible to read. They're really, really difficult, and I think that there's a tendency to tune out at a certain point.''\par \par \uc2 These stories can also be difficult to tell. Ms. Dorbert, the Florida woman who was unable to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is often brought to tears while recounting her pregnancy. And yet she has done so many times, including to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  in February 2023, to CNN and The Post again in May 2023, to the television host Katie Phang on MSNBC in May, to ''Good Morning America'' in June and to \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  magazine (and in a Florida political ad) in October.\par \par \uc2 Still, the 60-second \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video posted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America was the first time some viewers had heard her name.\par \par \uc2 On Oct. 28, four days after airing that clip, ''Morning Joe'' brought Ms. Dorbert to the program in person. She was part of a pretaped panel, alongside Ms. Richards, Ms. Joshua and other supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While introducing her guests, one of the show's hosts, \uc2 Mika Brzezinski\uc2 , already had tears in her eyes.\par \par \uc2 ''When I first aired this video on 'Morning Joe,' I was sitting at a table of five men,'' Ms. Brzezinski said in an email after taping the segment. ''They all cringed, and two of them cried.''\par \par \uc2 The panel aired one week before the election. Ms. Richards says she is concerned about what might happen when the election is over -- when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer a ballot measure or a candidate's core issue -- ''and all the money and press and attention to these stories will abate.''\par \par \uc2 ''Not because people have fatigue, because every day there's another story,'' she said. ''But because it's just hard to keep track of.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241102ekb20004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
