\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Contradictions And Ambiguity\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margot Sanger-Katz, Claire Cain Miller and Eve Washington \par \uc2 1,784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has softened his rhetoric, contradicted himself and nearly dropped ''pro-life'' from his vocabulary.\par \par \uc2 When he first ran for president, Donald J. Trump talked repeatedly about his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''I'm pro-life, and I was originally pro-choice,'' he said in 2016. Another time that year, he said, ''I am pro-life, and I will be appointing pro-life judges.'' In total in 2016, according to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis, he described himself as ''pro-life'' 36 times:\par \par \uc2 In this campaign, though, he hardly ever uses the term. This year, he has described himself as ''pro-life'' once.\par \par \uc2 During his re-election campaign in 2020, Mr. Trump often spoke of his support for a federal ban on abortions after 20 weeks of pregnancy. In total, before the Supreme Court decision that ended the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, he expressed his support for a federal ban 50 times.\par \par \uc2 Since the court's decision, Dobbs v. Jackson, which allowed individual states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he has expressed support for a federal ban only once, saying, ''It could be a state ban, it could be a federal ban.''\par \par \uc2 More often, he has said the exact opposite, that he opposes a federal ban. Since the court's decision, he has said that 11 times: ''I'm not signing a ban, and there's no reason to sign a ban''; ''There will not be a federal ban''; ''I'm not signing a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill.''\par \par \uc2 We looked at all the statements Mr. Trump has made about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since he first ran for president, in speeches, interviews and posts online, that were cataloged by Roll Call Factba.se. We categorized 660 of them based on the words he used. The analysis shows how his messaging over the last decade has changed with the political moment -- and how this year he has tried to distance himself from the core beliefs of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 There is no evidence that Mr. Trump's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed. As president, he did more to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the United States than any other president, including by appointing three of the six Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe v. Wade. As recently as the September debate, he took credit, saying, ''I did a great service in doing it.'' Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have laid out a pathway for him to effectively ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide if he is re-elected.\par \par \uc2 As it became clear that the Dobbs decision was hurting some Republican candidates, however, he changed his messaging, sometimes directly contradicting himself. He said as much in April, when he released a video on Truth Social emphasizing that Republicans needed to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a way that would appeal to voters: ''You must follow your heart on this issue. But remember, you must also win elections.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's ambiguous or even contradictory statements can allow voters to hear whichever message they want to hear, political analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Karoline Leavitt, the Trump campaign's national press secretary, said that Mr. Trump had been consistent in his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that he had always supported the rights of states to make decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Jason Rapert, founder and president of the National Association of Christian Lawmakers, which supports a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, said, ''I think we should remember the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of 2016, and what he was saying from his heart, and he made good on those policy decisions.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rapert said he was ''extremely disappointed'' to hear Mr. Trump say he did not support a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. But he does not necessarily believe him, he said: ''I realized that he really is delivering messaging that came from many of the consultants around him.''\par \par \uc2 Celinda Lake\uc2 , a Democratic pollster, said Mr. Trump was giving mixed messages because he was trying to hold onto the support of evangelical voters while also trying to win back moderates who have turned against him because of his role in overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a winning strategy with evangelicals -- you never hear born-again Christians are defecting; they're not,'' Ms. Lake said. ''It's been a loss with suburban women, with younger women, with the moderate Republican women who now call themselves independents, and he's been trying to figure out a way to get them back.''\par \par \uc2 In her polling and focus groups, she said, many voters have never really believed that Mr. Trump opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . His recent rhetoric ''just reinforces that his heart's not really in it, he doesn't really believe this,'' she said. ''But on the other hand, the MAGA party does believe in it, and his political calculations are such that he did Dobbs and he will continue to do more.''\par \par \uc2 Trump's evolution\par \par \uc2 Before he became a national political figure, Mr. Trump described himself as ''very pro-choice,'' so in his initial run for president as a Republican, he repeatedly emphasized that he had abandoned that position.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump was courting the evangelical wing of the Republican Party, whose votes he needed. He promised to appoint ''pro-life judges'' to the Supreme Court. He made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions a staple of his 2016 campaign, speaking often about how important the issue was.\par \par \uc2 After he was elected, Mr. Trump kept many of his promises to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. He enacted several regulatory policies that limited funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and organizations that supported it. He appointed three justices to the Supreme Court from a list of candidates preapproved by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 As he approached his re-election campaign, he supported a bill in Congress that would have banned abortions nationwide after 20 weeks of pregnancy. The bill was a major theme of campaign rallies, and was featured in his 2020 State of the Union address and a speech that year at the March for Life, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's biggest annual event. He was the first sitting president to speak there.\par \par \uc2 He called on Congress to ''defend the dignity of life and to pass legislation prohibiting late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of children who can feel pain in their mother's womb.''\par \par \uc2 After the Dobbs decision overturning the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, Mr. Trump often celebrated his role in it: ''If you look at what we've done with Roe v. Wade, we did something that everyone said couldn't be done, and we got it done.''\par \par \uc2 At the presidential debate with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in September, he again praised Dobbs, and ''the genius and heart and strength'' of the Supreme Court justices who supported it.\par \par \uc2 Later that month, he extolled his record on the issue: ''I've done an unbelievable job on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question.''\par \par \uc2 He often said that all Americans or ''all legal scholars'' supported the court's decision to overturn Roe, despite polling majorities and numerous legal briefs that opposed it.\par \par \uc2 During this campaign, though, he has sent mixed messages. At first, he said he would support a Florida measure expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, then said he wouldn't. He said he wouldn't restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, then said he was open to it.\par \par \uc2 He has not always directly answered questions about his personal views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Often, he avoids it by embracing the fact that Dobbs allowed states to restrict and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : ''It doesn't matter because this issue has now been taken over by the states.''\par \par \uc2 Before Dobbs, he did not say much about believing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be decided by the states. Now, he says it instead of answering questions about new federal policies he might pursue as president.\par \par \uc2 His party platform also emphasizes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are a decision for the states, saying only that the party opposes ''late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' Then it shifts to other topics, like support for prenatal care, and access to birth control and in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 He also deflects by saying the Democrats have taken extreme positions: ''The radicals are really the Democrats because they'll kill babies in their eighth and ninth month and they'll kill babies after birth.'' In fact, infanticide is illegal in every state. Arguments about Democratic extremism were also a staple of his 2020 re-election campaign speeches, but he is talking about that even more now.\par \par \uc2 He has also recently repeated his support for exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans for women who are victims of rape or incest, or whose lives are threatened by their pregnancies. These statements somewhat conflict with his embrace of state-based approaches to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, since many states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans do not have these exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Melania Trump, the former first lady, published a book this month in which she said she supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a surprise that some political analysts said was a calculated move to appeal to more moderate voters.\par \par \uc2 For much of this campaign, Mr. Trump declined to say whether he would veto a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Then, in recent weeks, he changed course again, saying that he would veto a federal ban, in direct opposition to the goals of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. He said in an online message earlier this month: ''Everyone knows I would not support a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, under any circumstances, and would, in fact, veto it, because it is up to the states to decide based on the will of their voters.''\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, Mr. Trump is not talking much about how he would handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from the executive branch. Regardless of who wins the election, major federal legislation on the topic is unlikely to pass Congress in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he has begun saying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not an important issue: ''The country's falling apart,'' he said recently. ''We're going to end up in World War III, and all they can talk about is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That's all they talk about. And it really no longer pertains because we've done something on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that nobody thought was possible.''\par \par \uc2 But the president still has major influence on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide, through regulations and executive actions. In Mr. Trump's last administration, he cut federal funding to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for contraceptive services. He appointed numerous federal judges who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. If elected again, he could also use the Food and Drug Administration or the Justice Department to ban or restrict the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, which have contributed to increased \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access since Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 ''I know he's steering clear with only days left until the election,'' said Mr. Rapert of the National Association of Christian Lawmakers, ''but clearly the federal government can do more.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241102ekb20002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
