\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Late Abortions Are Rare, But They Still Dominate The Debate in Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Both sides are trying to make their opponents sound extreme. Here is what studies and data show about when and why abortions happen later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 More than 80 percent of abortions in the United States happen before 10 weeks, in the embryonic stage of pregnancy. But in the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the arguments and almost all of the ads focus on the other end, on the much rarer abortions later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 This has never been more evident, or consequential, than this year. It's the first presidential election year since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Ten states are voting on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures, including states that are battlegrounds for the presidency and control of Congress, and polls show that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has newly energized Democrats and women.\par \par \uc2 The closing weeks of the campaign have become a race to paint the other side as more extreme.\par \par \uc2 Republicans and groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights argue that Democrats are pushing abortions ''for any reason'' in the seventh, eighth and ninth month of pregnancy, even after birth. ''Jon Tester supports aborting a healthy, full-term baby the day before it's due,'' Tim Sheehy, the Republican challenger for Senate in Montana says, falsely, on his campaign website. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  went further into inaccuracies, saying Democrats would allow women to ''execute the baby.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats and sponsors of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures have leaned into graphic stories of women with much-wanted pregnancies who sought abortions in medical emergencies or because of fetal anomalies diagnosed late in pregnancy. In stark ads, women tell of being denied abortions until they were close to death. ''I remember the doctor handing me a baby boy that was blue, and I just held him, because he was so cold,'' says Deborah, a Florida woman who carried a pregnancy to 37 weeks after being told at 23 weeks the child would not survive. ''This ban is torture.''\par \par \uc2 Both sides play to emotion and ambivalence: Polls show that Americans overwhelmingly support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first trimester, but, beyond that, their answer is some version of ''it depends'' -- on why women seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and how late.\par \par \uc2 Talking about late abortions has long been especially persuasive: In the mid-1990s, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents campaigned against a rare second-trimester procedure they called ''partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' -- describing fetuses dismembered, their skulls crushed -- polls showed a sudden, sharp drop in support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even among women and Democrats. The number of Americans identifying as ''pro-life'' rose.\par \par \uc2 The opposite has happened in the two years since the court overturned Roe, with polls showing that voters are more likely to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures if they have heard stories about women with pregnancy complications forced to travel out of state -- that's true even among Republicans and those who say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in most cases.\par \par \uc2 ''The more granular you get in terms of threats to women's health, at every trimester, the more support you're going to find for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Lydia Saad, a pollster at Gallup who has written about the effect of talking about abortions later in pregnancy. ''The more graphic you get about the development of the fetus or the procedure, the more pushback you're going to get. That's why the issue is so potent.''\par \par \uc2 There is no evidence that abortions are happening right up until birth, much less after -- at that stage, doctors say it is a delivery of a live child, and infanticide, which is illegal everywhere. It's also true, as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists say, that not all later abortions are in cases of medical emergencies or fetal abnormalities. But there's also no evidence to support their charge that these cases happen regularly, with women aborting healthy but inconvenient pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  reports that about 94 percent of abortions happen before the end of the first trimester, at or before 13 weeks. Less than 1 percent happen after 21 weeks (in the fifth month).\par \par \uc2 That percentage has actually declined over the last decade, as tests now detect pregnancy earlier, and more women use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the early weeks.\par \par \uc2 Last year, there were an estimated 9,300 abortions after 21 weeks. But the \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  and most states do not specify exactly when after 21 weeks those abortions happen -- whether they are at, say, 22 weeks, or 36 weeks -- or the reasons women sought them. That leaves a huge gap for hypotheticals, just at the point in pregnancy that polls show Americans begin to qualify their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most states prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, when the fetus can survive outside the uterus; with medical technology, that's now about 24 weeks. A fetus is ''at term,'' meaning it can survive on its own without intervention, at 37 weeks. And ''late-term'' refers to pregnancies that go beyond the usual 40 weeks -- meaning that ''late-term abortions'' do not happen.\par \par \uc2 Viability was the standard the Supreme Court set in Roe, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures seek to reinstate that same threshold.\par \par \uc2 But even if someone wanted to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond viability, it's difficult and expensive to find a place that will do it. Only 14 facilities in the United States publicly offer abortions beyond 24 weeks of pregnancy. Most of those stop at 26 weeks, three go until 28 weeks and one, in Colorado, until 32 weeks. Women have to find an appointment and arrange and pay for last-minute travel, not to mention the cost of the late procedure, which itself can cost from a few thousand dollars to $25,000.\par \par \uc2 This means that not many abortions happen after viability, even in states with no gestational limit. In Michigan, for example, where voters enshrined a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution in 2022, data for the next year shows only two abortions after 24 weeks: one between 25 and 28 weeks, and one after that.\par \par \uc2 There was one post-viability \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ohio last year, sometime between 28 and 36 weeks. In Minnesota, there were two in 2022, one at 25 weeks and one at 32 weeks. And in Texas, there were eight abortions between 23 and 26 weeks in 2020, the last year before a ban took effect, and none later.\par \par \uc2 Most states do not report the reasons for these abortions. But in Florida, which does, there have been three post-viability abortions this year: one in a case of a ''serious'' fetal abnormality and two because of fatal abnormalities. In the four previous years, there were no abortions past 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The outlier is Colorado, where there were 137 abortions at or beyond 28 weeks -- the start of the seventh month -- in 2023. Of those, the state says most were between weeks 28 and 29; 8 percent, or roughly 10 abortions, were at 32 weeks or beyond.\par \par \uc2 Colorado is home to the clinic run by Dr. Warren Hern, the only provider in the country who publicly offers abortions after 32 weeks. Now 86, Dr. Hern has been doing abortions since Roe and fiercely defends a woman's right to choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage of pregnancy; he specializes in late procedures, and many of his patients travel from other states. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists hold him up as proof that third trimester abortions do happen, and not always because of dire medical diagnoses.\par \par \uc2 In particular, they cite two papers he published, in 2005 and 2014, and a profile of him in The Atlantic, where he said he had done an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for sex selection.\par \par \uc2 But they misquote those papers to argue that just 20 percent or 30 percent of the abortions he has performed late in pregnancy were for medical reasons. In fact, those percentages include abortions done earlier in pregnancy, before viability.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Dr. Hern said that the abortions he does after 28 weeks are for serious fetal anomalies or because the woman's life is at risk, and that ''generally speaking'' he would not provide one after 33 weeks, ''and not if she just doesn't want'' a child.\par \par \uc2 ''These are tough calls, and I'm here to help people, but I'm not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  dispensing machine,'' he said. ''I'm not going to give it to anybody who walks in the door.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists also try to make their case using two papers by Diana Greene Foster and Katrina Kimport of the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 . Timothy P. Carney, a senior fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  quoted them to argue last year that ''many abortions after 20 weeks happen without dire diagnoses for the mother or baby,'' and that ''there is plenty of evidence that elective late-term abortions happen every day in the United States.''\par \par \uc2 Yet these researchers, too, say their work has been misinterpreted and does not support the claim that many late abortions terminate healthy pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights seize in particular on a line in a study Dr. Kimport published in 2022, looking at why women have abortions in the third trimester. One reason was that they had obtained new information. And, in the sentence anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists like to quote, ''For some respondents, the new information they obtained was that they were pregnant.''\par \par \uc2 But for most women in the study, the new information was a medical diagnosis that could not be detected earlier in pregnancy. The study notes that only a ''small subset'' of women do not find out they are pregnant until the third trimester. They often have no reason to believe they are pregnant: They have no symptoms, or continue to get regular periods. And the study was based on interviews with only 28 women -- not designed to be representative.\par \par \uc2 ''They can't make these claims based on their own efforts because there isn't evidence of their claims,'' Dr. Kimport said in an interview. ''If this was something that was so rampant, they ought to be able to find data.''\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, polls show support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising -- even for abortions in the second and third trimester. Most Americans still qualify that support. ''But if you're going to force them to choose,'' said Ms. Saad, at Gallup, ''they will choose legal with no restrictions rather than make it illegal for everyone.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241101ekb10003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
