\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Policing,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Trump: Inside a Tightening Race for a Key House Seat\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tracey Tully \par \uc2 1,157 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sue Altman, a Democrat, has focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  as she seeks to unseat a Republican incumbent, Representative Thomas Kean Jr., in a New Jersey swing district.\par \par \uc2 Sue Altman has made a name for herself by taking on political heavy hitters in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 First was \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , the famously pugnacious Republican governor, who, during a 2016 town hall, was so exasperated by her questions about education funding that he tossed his microphone to her.\par \par \uc2 Three years later, she tangled with \uc2 George Norcross III\uc2 , then among the state's most influential Democratic power brokers, as she led a drumbeat of criticism against corporate tax breaks awarded to companies with close ties to him.\par \par \uc2 Now Ms. Altman is seeking to unseat Thomas Kean Jr., a first-term Republican congressman who is the scion and namesake of a former governor, in one of a handful of races nationwide that will determine whether Republicans retain control of the House.\par \par \uc2 The result of Tuesday's election in New Jersey's Seventh Congressional District may say a lot about how Mr. Kean, 56, has campaigned in the race, where recent polls have prompted Democrats to mount a last-minute push in hopes of flipping the seat.\par \par \uc2 But it also may offer insight into the direction of New Jersey and of suburban swing districts like the Seventh, an affluent and well-educated region split nearly evenly between Republicans and Democrats. President Biden beat \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  there by four points in 2020, but two years later Mr. Kean beat the Democratic incumbent, \uc2 Tom Malinowski\uc2 , by about three points.\par \par \uc2 Ben Dworkin, director of Rowan University's Institute for Public Policy and Citizenship, said he saw the election mainly as a referendum on the hold that Mr. Trump has on the moderate wing of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ''If Democrats are able to win in NJ-7,'' Professor Dworkin said, ''then it will really show the negative impact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had on Republican candidates down ballot.''\par \par \uc2 Republican PACs have poured millions of dollars into the race. Voters in some parts of the North Jersey district, which stretches from the state's border with Pennsylvania through quaint towns filled with horse farms to the eastern cities of Rahway and Linden, say their mailboxes fill regularly with Kean campaign literature.\par \par \uc2 According to polls, Mr. Kean is ahead by about two points against Ms. Altman, a former teacher and professional basketball player who until last year ran the Working Families alliance, a left-leaning political organizing group.\par \par \uc2 The campaign arm of House Democrats committed to spending about $4 million just last week -- its first financial commitment in the race -- after surveys showed that the contest was narrowing. Democratic Party luminaries like Representative \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland, a leader of the congressional progressive PAC, and Bill Bradley, a former U.S. senator and \uc2 New York Knicks\uc2  star, have joined Ms. Altman, 42, on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 At a candidate forum last week, the race's high stakes and the potency of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a political issue were on display.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kean described Ms. Altman's support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand,'' the same terminology he used two years ago in his successful campaign against Mr. Malinowski.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Altman supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and a New Jersey law that codified the right to the procedure throughout a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kean, who has endorsed Mr. Trump, describes himself as ''pro-choice'' and said he would oppose a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But he has also said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was ''best handled at the state level'' when asked about the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, which ended a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During the forum, Mr. Kean, who opposed funding for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  while in the State Legislature, twice referred to doctors who ''commit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' When asked about the phrasing, which could be interpreted as condemning doctors who perform abortions, he said he had misspoken.\par \par \uc2 Registered Democrats outnumber Republicans in New Jersey by 917,000 voters.\par \par \uc2 But that is not the case in the Seventh Congressional District, where the G.O.P. has more support and people who are not registered with either major party represent the largest bloc of voters. The district is filled with fiscally conservative residents who also support moderate social policies and who helped to propel Republicans like Mr. Christie; Mr. Kean's father, Gov. \uc2 Thomas Kean Sr\uc2 .; and \uc2 Christine Todd Whitman\uc2  to the state's highest office.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll that put Mr. Kean ahead by two percentage points also found that more voters trusted him to handle issues related to the economy and crime. That edge was dwarfed, however, by Ms. Altman's advantage when voters were asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kean and his campaign have emphasized the brand of liberal politics that Ms. Altman stood for while leading Working Families, one of the state's most progressive organizations. He has also highlighted a comment she made in 2020 on social media that referred to ''those of us working on #DefundThePolice in Jersey.''\par \par \uc2 She has apologized for that sentiment and disavowed it, saying she has since gained a better understanding of policing from officers and family members who worked for police agencies.\par \par \uc2 But it is an issue that has resonated. At a town-hall meeting Ms. Altman held in Phillipsburg, on the state's western flank, a member of the audience, Vinny Panico, challenged her position on policing.\par \par \uc2 ''We have people breaking into houses, rummaging through people's personal belongings to find their car keys to steal their cars,'' Mr. Panico, 33, a Republican, said in a subsequent interview, referring to a yearslong uptick in car thefts. ''Our police departments, and our men and women in blue, and our justice system, need support.''\par \par \uc2 Like Mr. Kean, Ms. Altman has worked to appeal to centrist voters, or, as she describes them, ''moderate people who are witnessing their beloved Republican Party dwindling down to this extremist morass.''\par \par \uc2 She grew up in the district, regularly mentions that her parents are Republicans, and in stump speeches stresses her background as an ''anti-corruption advocate'' with a record of opposing Democratic as well as Republican leaders.\par \par \uc2 Both candidates are highly educated and each has raised more than $5 million in campaign contributions. Ms. Altman has two master's degrees from Oxford; Mr. Kean has a master's from \uc2 Tufts\uc2  and completed his doctoral studies there.\par \par \uc2 But the comparisons end there.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kean is an awkward public speaker, holds few events open broadly to the public and avoids most media requests for interviews.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Altman's energy and candor are among the first things people note after listening to her speak.\par \par \uc2 ''To me, she's like a breath of fresh air,'' Melissa Lipman, 78, a Democrat, said after an event last week.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241031ekav0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
