\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Right Spends Heavily on New York  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Measure\b0\par\par\uc2 By Grace Ashford \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Opponents of the so-called Equal Rights Amendment have cast it as a broad attempt by Democrats to grant rights to transgender athletes and migrants.\par \par \uc2 A late-stage effort to defeat a New York State ballot measure that would enshrine a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the State Constitution has been bolstered by $8 million in donations from a handful of conservative donors.\par \par \uc2 The Vote No on Prop 1 political action committee received $6.5 million from Dick Uihlein, a scion of one of the founders of Schlitz beer and the founder of the shipping company Uline. Along with his wife, Mr. Uihlein has given generously to former President Donald J. Trump, as well as to groups opposed to gay and transgender rights. Last year, Mr. Uihlein spent $4 million to defeat Ohio's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment, providing the bulk of the funding against the measure.\par \par \uc2 The committee also received $1 million from Thomas J. Tisch, a financier who was a key supporter of Lee Zeldin's unsuccessful bid for governor of New York in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The late infusion of cash is expected to amplify opponents' messaging surrounding the measure, known as the Equal Rights Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Conceived of as a way to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the fall of Roe v. Wade, the initiative would also expand legal protections to people based on sexual orientation, gender identity, age, disability and national origin.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had hoped that the ballot initiative could help boost turnout by energizing voters who care about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Public sentiment in New York appeared to be on the ballot's side: A recent Siena College poll shows that some 69 percent of New Yorkers approve of the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Party leaders pledged to raise $20 million to support the initiative, but as Election Day draws near, they have struggled to come anywhere close to that. Much of the money raised by New Yorkers for Equal Rights, a PAC that supports the amendment, has gone to pay for consultants rather than for political ads.\par \par \uc2 Sasha Ahuja, the PAC's campaign director, rejected assertions that the group had in any way mismanaged its funds, saying in a statement that the group would spend $3.1 million in paid advertising between the PAC and the state party. ''We are using the full force of our organizing muscle on doors, on the phone and other text in every corner of the state,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 But the influx of donations and the coordinated spending campaign from the Vote No on Prop 1 committee has reignited proponents' fears that the initiative could be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 In the past week, the Vote No committee spent $5 million on a burst of television, digital and radio ads, urging voters to reject the amendment, according to state Board of Elections filings.\par \par \uc2 The committee's recent ad blitz seeks to harness voter frustration over immigration issues to make a case against the amendment. Over footage of an unruly mob, the ad warns that the amendment would extend taxpayer benefits to undocumented people and even ''open the door to noncitizens voting.''\par \par \uc2 ''This isn't about equal rights,'' an announcer says. ''It's about special rights for illegal immigrants.''\par \par \uc2 And while the amendment's authors and the New York City Bar Association agree that the amendment would not give undocumented immigrants the right to vote, that has not stopped opponents from their claims.\par \par \uc2 Another group, the Coalition to Protect Kids, has spent a little less than $500,000 against the amendment, which it says would destroy girls sports by allowing transgender girls to participate.\par \par \uc2 The Vote No committee also received $500,000 from Warren Stephens of Little Rock, Ark., $25,000 from Sean Fieler of Stamford, Conn., and $100 from Allen Roth of New York City.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241029ekat0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
