\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Big Checklist for the Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Movement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,961 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Activists on both sides say Trump could effectively ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide and establish fetal personhood, the longtime goal of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Trump\uc2  did this,'' the campaign ads for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  declare.\par \par \uc2 ''This'' was appointing three Supreme Court justices who helped overturn Roe v. Wade, paving the way for the state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that have led to often harrowing experiences for many pregnant women denied care.\par \par \uc2 As seismic as that was, activists on both sides of the debate believe a newly elected President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  could go further to effectively ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide -- and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have given him the road map.\par \par \uc2 It is hard to know what on the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda Mr. Trump would support. He has shifted his public views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so often that it's hard to say what he believes or wants to do. He has said he wants to ''leave it to the states.'' More recently, he said he would not sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and would veto one if a Republican-controlled Congress sent it to his desk. He has publicly supported I.V.F., which some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups oppose.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Trump would also be under pressure from the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that have ardently supported him to take other actions that would go much further than the 15-week national bans that congressional Republicans have proposed since Roe was overturned. These include reversing federal guidance that says even states with bans must allow doctors to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of medical emergency; using administrative agencies to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills; and using his executive powers to achieve the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's ultimate goal: recognizing fetal personhood in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Those actions would not require cooperation from Congress, and could play out in the far reaches of the federal bureaucracy by Trump appointees. While any changes would almost certainly be challenged in court, the judges who hear those cases could be Trump appointees, existing or new.\par \par \uc2 ''There are levers that he and his appointees can pull, way short of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  much more difficult -- if not illegal -- in all the states where it's legal still,'' said \uc2 David S. Cohen\uc2 , a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2  who has written extensively about federal regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those moves could effectively override state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures that voters have passed since the court reversed Roe two years ago -- and any of the 10 state ballot measures that pass in November.\par \par \uc2 The easiest and perhaps most consequential move, and the one the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is pushing hardest, would be enforcing the Comstock Act, which is long dormant but still on the books. That law, enacted in 1873, made it a federal crime to send or receive materials ''designed, adapted, or intended'' for ''obscene'' or ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -causing'' purposes.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have pushed to use the Comstock Act to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. They also say that the law would criminalize the delivery or receipt of medical instruments used in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Enforcing it, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents say, would go a long way toward stopping what they see as the murder of a million unborn children each year. ''We don't need a federal ban when we have Comstock on the books,'' Jonathan F. Mitchell, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyer, told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  earlier this year. ''There's a smorgasbord of options.''\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , and a historian of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , agrees that the act could give Mr. Trump enormous power.\par \par \uc2 ''That's not what I think the Comstock Act means, but it's promising as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents see it, because it doesn't require voters, it doesn't require Congress -- it just requires the Supreme Court and the executive branch,'' Professor Ziegler said. ''The 15-week minimum standard is not what the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement wants, anyway. Comstock gets them a lot closer to what they want.''\par \par \uc2 The way anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists interpret Comstock, she said, the act would not allow exceptions for abortions if a woman's physical or mental health were at risk, or in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 And, she added, ''Part of what's exciting to people in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement about Comstock is that it doesn't have to stop there.''\par \par \uc2 As interpreted by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers, Professor Ziegler said, the act could be used to criminalize buying or selling medications used in gender-affirming treatment or to prevent H.I.V. infections -- which Mr. Mitchell and his colleagues in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement have tried to restrict.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mitchell also helped Mr. Trump with his lawsuit to stay on the ballot in Colorado. If he or other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers and judges who have supported Mr. Trump are rewarded with positions in the administration, Professor Cohen said, ''even if they never say a word about Comstock, the threat of it goes way up, and the risk goes way up.''\par \par \uc2 That could cause a chilling effect among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, much the way doctors in states like Wisconsin, Michigan and Arizona stopped providing abortions after Roe was overturned because of century-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that were still on the books.\par \par \uc2 ''Providers are going to have to sit there wondering if they are going to drop this hammer at any point in time,'' Professor Cohen said.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have also drafted a blueprint for how Mr. Trump could use his executive powers to recognize fetal personhood in the Constitution -- meaning that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any stage would qualify as murder.\par \par \uc2 Americans United For Life\uc2  outlined this idea in a 2021 document called the Lincoln Proposal. Much as President Lincoln used his constitutional powers to abolish slavery despite the Supreme Court's affirmation of it in the Dred Scott decision, the group called on Mr. Trump to issue an executive order ''recognizing preborn persons as constitutional 'persons' entitled to the fundamental human rights of due process and equal protection of the laws'' safeguarded in the Fourteenth Amendment.\par \par \uc2 This is why anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups celebrated the mention of the Fourteenth Amendment in the Republican platform this summer, even as the party removed explicit support for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which is unpopular among voters.\par \par \uc2 ''The pro-life generation wants to make a New Deal with President Trump and the Republican Party,'' said \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , the president of Students for Life, ''to ensure that future generations get a chance at life in America, and we stop a weaponized federal government from pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wherever it can.''\par \par \uc2 But much depends on how much of an appetite Republicans, including Mr. Trump, have to pursue anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies after nearly three years of being hammered on the issue by voters.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Republicans badly underperformed in the 2022 midterm elections, and since then have tried to distance themselves from the kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans they proposed just two years ago -- as has Mr. Trump. If they don't believe they have paid a penalty on Election Day, they might be inclined to help Mr. Trump enact more anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump might decide, as he has said on occasion, that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have already gone too far. Or, he might feel indebted to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who have supported him, and appoint them to key positions.\par \par \uc2 The Lincoln Proposal cites the work of Dr. Maureen L. Condic, a Trump appointee to the National Science Board, an advisory panel to the \uc2 National Science Foundation\uc2 , who is a neurobiology professor and bioethicist who has long lobbied against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, using disputed science. A second term could give other Trump appointees the power to shape the science around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in turn influences court decisions.\par \par \uc2 And although Mr. Trump recently declared himself the ''father of I.V.F.,'' enacting fetal personhood could endanger in vitro fertilization. Fertility clinics dispose of unused embryos, and some clinics stopped operating temporarily earlier this year in the confusion following an Alabama court ruling that had declared those embryos to be children.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump could also reverse the Biden administration's guidance on a federal law known as EMTALA that requires hospitals that receive federal funds to provide patients with stabilizing care. The administration, through its appointees at the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , said that even states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have to allow doctors to provide them in cases of medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 ''That would be the first thing to go, because it doesn't require anything other than their interpretation,'' Professor Cohen said. ''It didn't have to go through rule-making, it was just guidance from the department.''\par \par \uc2 The guidance from the Biden administration was intended to ease what doctors have described as the chaos that has often resulted from state bans, with pregnant women who are miscarrying or in other medical emergencies turned away from hospitals and told they need to get sicker before they can have abortions.\par \par \uc2 Guidance from the Trump administration might say that there is no circumstance in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is medically necessary -- the argument that many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists make, saying that women should carry pregnancies until the fetus or child dies on its own.\par \par \uc2 Trump guidance could go further, to say that EMTALA requires hospitals to treat the ''unborn child'' as a patient and to take measures to save its life, as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers have argued before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ''You could imagine a Trump administration issuing guidance saying that hospitals in blue states could not intervene to do abortions because EMTALA treats the unborn as patient,'' Ms. Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Mr. Trump could use executive powers to deny Title X funding to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , as he did in his first administration -- a decision that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  reversed.\par \par \uc2 Then there is the Food and Drug Administration. To try to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access during Covid shutdowns, the Biden administration allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be delivered by mail, eliminating a requirement that women visit a clinic or doctor to get the first of two pills.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have sued to reverse that decision. Project 2025, drafted as a blueprint for a second administration but disavowed by Mr. Trump, argues to go further and to revoke the F.D.A.'s approval of one of the pills, mifepristone, which would remove the drug from the market entirely.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has also stepped up enforcement of the Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances Act, which prohibits ''threats of force, obstruction and property damage'' to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. As the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics has declined, the remaining clinics have reported an increase in protesters. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups argue that the Biden administration has used the law to harass them, and they could push Mr. Trump to halt prosecutions under it.\par \par \uc2 Of course, if Mr. Trump were to win the election, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups would likely be flooded with new donations and would fight any new restrictions in court. But perhaps the biggest legacy of Mr. Trump's first term was how he shaped the federal courts. He could do so even more in a second term.\par \par \uc2 At the Supreme Court, Justices \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  are rumored to be close to retirement, opening up the possibility of a court with a majority of Trump appointees. Among the names on Mr. Trump's list of potential nominees are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents including Judge James Ho of the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals and Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  of Missouri.\par \par \uc2 ''We all obsessed about judges when the question was, Will they overturn Roe,'' Professor Ziegler said. ''Once they overturned Roe, we stopped paying attention, but people in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement didn't stop paying attention.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241029ekat0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
