\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Stories From Post-Roe America\b0\par\par\uc2 3,962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 What does it really mean to live in a country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer a constitutional right? Since 2022, when the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, many states have made it all but impossible to get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care within their borders, and have done their best to isolate people facing unwanted or complicated pregnancies, making them afraid to reach out to medical providers or even to friends and loved ones who might help them. New laws have forced doctors to delay care in life-threatening situations and made women afraid to seek it, leading to preventable deaths. Even when the consequences haven't been that dire, the day-to-day reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America's left-behind places now involves navigating constant undercurrents of confusion and fear: Is this pill I found on the internet safe? If I miscarry, is anyone going to help me? Or, in the cases of some doctors: How can I help this patient without getting arrested?\par \par \uc2  We asked people at the front lines of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to help us understand how the new laws have filtered into their worlds. The stories we found were ones of women getting access -- just barely. Of doctors and volunteers white-knuckling it to provide the support, funding and care required. But there are many stories of women who don't manage to navigate the chaos, who get lost in the fault lines. As recently reported by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , shortly after Roe was overturned, a woman in Georgia died as a result of delayed access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . How many more of her are out there? How many more will there be if we continue down this path? This presidential election will be the first since the Supreme Court ruled on Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, when voters will be presented with a choice: to try to rein in the chaos unleashed by the ruling or to live in a country that continues to be shaped by it. Rev. Erika Ferguson The head of Tubman Travel Project, a group that shepherds women on flights out of Texas to receive legal abortions. [Women] are traveling with me by commercial airline into another state to receive the care that they cannot get here in the state of Texas. I don't know what they look like, or what their backgrounds are. I don't even actually know what procedures they're having. I do know that I'm taking them to get the reproductive care they need, and that typically is an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care was always a solo venture. Texas, particularly, has made it a siloed venture, where women are scared to even talk to their family about what they need. We are able to change all of that. And that's really subversive in a way, because they become each other's confidants through shared experience. Aiding and abetting -- if that is what the courts find it to be, if something happens because of that, then my work is well worth it, because I've seen what happens for women when they get a chance to get the care they need and get back to the lives that they want. Christina A 35-year-old mother in Texas who learned her fetus had a severe chromosomal abnormality. Around the 12-week mark I get offered something called the genetic blood test. About a week and a half later, I get a call on a Monday that my genetic blood tests came back abnormal. It flagged for trisomy 21, which is well known for Down syndrome. We were in the more severe category. Then we had a very extensive ultrasound done, and this is when we discovered that there were cardiac defects coming along. I said, well, what about an elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for incompatibility of life and medical complications? The OB said, ''You would have that option out of state, but you would have to conduct that on your own to do that.'' The conversation had to end there. I found that we had an 80 to 90 percent risk of miscarriage with this pregnancy. And if we didn't miscarry that he would only survive most likely hours or days after birth. So I kind of looked at my options here as far as: OK, so if I miscarry, what does that look like? If I end up bleeding too much, I have to come to the E.R. and wait at the E.R.; they still can't intervene. It has to become lifethreatening. So I understood it like you're not going to intervene, you're not going to step in and do the common-sense thing. You will have to deliver that dead fetus. That was pretty appalling for me. My husband, my daughter, me, we get on an airplane. We travel from Texas to Boston, then we have to drive another hour and a half up to New Hampshire to settle with my family. Should something go wrong, he dies before we travel, it's a four-and-a-half-hour flight. I brought gloves. I brought a blood pressure cuff in case I did start miscarrying. I was just prepared for the worst. It's tedious, it's exhausting. I'm not in the comfort of my own home, my own bed. I'm dragging my entire family, uprooting them just so that I can get adequate care and that I can be safe. Dr. Andrea Palmer An OB-GYN in Fort Worth, where she has seen an uptick in the number of patients asking to be sterilized. I just finished doing a laparoscopic salpingectomy on a young woman, 23 years old, who has not had children and desires not to have children. This is one in a string of many sterilizations I've done on young women without children since S.B. 8 passed in Texas and Dobbs passed at the level of the Supreme Court. There has been a movement by young women who do not have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who do not want to be pregnant, who are opting for surgical sterilization when they can find somebody to do it for them. I'm probably doing one to two of these a month now, where, prior to 2022, I would maybe have one of these a year. So it's been a huge shift. Cole A health care worker in Iowa. Cole had been trying to get pregnant through I.V.F. in Iowa and learned her fetus had a genetic abnormality around the same time her state upheld a ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. The doctor immediately shut down. I think she was trying to prepare me for how serious this was. I asked her, ''This sounds like I'm probably going to have to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the child doesn't die in utero.'' We talked about how that would even be possible. [Ed. note: The Iowa Supreme Court reinstated a six-week ban on abortions in June 2024. It would be another month before the ban went into effect.] She said, ''Well, if I was you, I would start calling clinics.'' She said, ''I think you're going to have to go to Illinois.'' I'm not only devastated about the diagnosis, I'm also trying to figure out how to just get health care. And so I kind of started panicking. I tried with multiple academic hospitals or larger hospital systems. They were inundated with calls. Some of them were like, ''OK, well, we want you to come two weeks from now. Come to an appointment, and then we'll have you come back for the procedure.'' The thought of that was just sickening, because at this point I was 12 and a half weeks pregnant. And so it was, like, we're talking about 15 weeks, I'm going to be doing this. It didn't feel fair to know what I knew about the pregnancy and continue the pregnancy. I go to my appointment with the maternal fetal medicine doctor. The doctor and the geneticist and basically everyone else -- I mean, this is at this point, like, seven people in the room -- said they would do exactly the same thing and that they agreed with the decision, and I'm not sure how or what happened, but the doctor came back with paperwork and said, ''We can perform the D and C here.'' Sometimes I worry about the doctor. Could she get in trouble for that decision that she made, which, to me, didn't really even feel like a decision? Because, I mean, we were essentially waiting for a baby to die. Dr. Kristl Tomlin One of the few pediatric gynecologists in South Carolina willing to provide abortions to children who qualified within the legal exceptions of rape or incest. After a series of troubling interactions with her local sheriff's office, she moved to a state with fewer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. [My patient] was a 13-year-old kid. She had been walking down the street and this guy pulled up in the car and he said, ''Hey, you want to get in the car? I'll buy you some food.'' And she went, ''OK.'' He took her to his apartment, where he locked her in and he raped her. The neighbors heard her screaming and they called the police and the police battered down the door and caught this guy in the act and arrested him, thankfully, and took her to the emergency room. As part of her follow-up, she had a positive pregnancy test a couple of weeks later. When she came to my office, she'd had an ultrasound because this is part of this law. There has to be because this emphasis is on fetal heart rate -- you can only assess that through ultrasound. Except through ultrasound, at small gestational ages like that, you typically need to do a transvaginal ultrasound. That is a probe that is stuck up your vagina and moved around until we can get the images that we need. Not amazing at the best of times, but definitely not amazing when you're a child who's just been abducted and raped. The gestational age corresponded exactly to the day this kid was rescued by the police. So there is zero ambiAbortion Stories From Post-Roe America New York Times Opinion Reported by Meher Ahmad, Lindsay Crouse, Vishakha Darbha, Amy Littlefield, Tenzin D. Tsagong and Jillian Weinberger. ARTWORK BY CARMEN WINANT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; SOURCE PHOTOGRAPHS BY LUISA CONLON, ISABEL CASTRO AND CARMEN WINANT Christina had to leave Texas to receive care after learning her wanted pregnancy had fetal abnormalities. Chelsea flew from Tennessee to Maryland after experiencing complications with her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. C M Y K NYxx,2024-10-27,SR,011,Cs-4C,E1 THE NEW YORK TIMES, SUNDAY, OCTOBER 27, 2024 SR 11 OPINION guity that this is a pregnancy as a result of rape. And so we did it. Great. Now I have to call the sheriff. I said, ''Hi, I'm Dr. Tomlin. I just need to call and report that I performed this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a victim of rape.'' And she goes, ''Oh. Well, you're not going to be able to do that this way.'' And I said, ''Excuse me?'' She goes, ''We'll send a uniformed officer to your location.'' And I said, ''No, no, no, no, no, no, no. That's not how this works.'' And she goes, ''Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes. That's how it's going to work.'' I went home. I'm making dinner, and I get a phone call to my cellphone. ''Hi, this is Officer Whatever. I'm on my way to your location. I just want to confirm.'' And I was like, ''Oh, no, you're not. You are not coming.'' My husband came home, and I'm drinking a glass of wine. I'm shaking. And he was like, ''What happened? Are you OK?'' I kind of told him and my older daughter -- she would have been 10 at the time -- she overheard me, and she started crying, and she said, ''Mommy, are police coming to the house? Are they coming to arrest you?'' I was like, ''No, they're not coming to arrest me.'' But maybe they were. I don't know. [The sheriff's department did not respond to questions from The New York Times about their attempt to visit Dr. Tomlin.] All of a sudden, I'd had these encounters with the police who had felt completely emboldened to treat me like dirt because now their legislators had made it seem like I was some criminal for providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nothing here is going to change that's going to make this better. This isn't a blip. This is the new reality. And I don't know that Dobbs is ever going to change in my career. I may retire in a world where restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is still the law of the land. It's not going to get better here. Dr. Amy Kelley An OB-GYN in South Dakota, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws affect her decision-making for her patients. A patient had been admitted from the E.R. and my partner felt like she probably had an infected uterus. This patient was 10 weeks pregnant. And so normally, if we think you have an infected uterus and you're pregnant, we recommend a D and C if you're early. My hospital bylaws, which are really there to try to protect us, say that we can only end a pregnancy if two physicians agree. In my state, you can only end a pregnancy if the woman's life is at stake. But in this case, my maternal fetal medicine specialist didn't think that she was sick enough. Myself and my general partner, we're pretty sure that's what was going on, and we're a smidge miffed. But at the same time, you can hardly be upset with our maternal fetal medicine colleague because she's right. We don't really know what our law means, and we don't really know if we'll be charged if some prosecutor decides that this patient wasn't sick enough. I can't even be mad at her because she's just trying to protect herself and her license. This patient's on antibiotics, so we watch her. About four hours later, she now has a temp of 105, which is a crazy, crazy temperature. Thank God her fetus doesn't have a heartbeat anymore. Isn't that terrible that I'm saying ''thank God''? But I'm saying that because then it's easy. Then we know we can do a D and C. We can empty her infected uterus. We can empty it and hopefully save her from going into septic shock. One of these days, we're going to wait too long, and God forbid, we lose a patient. I hope that never happens. But we know from history that it has before, and it could again. Nik A single mom from Texas who had to lie to her provider after taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills for fear of legal repercussions. Living in Texas, I knew that this was not going to be a simple journey. My best friend took charge, she called Planned Parenthood. They were very matter-of-fact: They were like, we cannot help you. We cannot do anything for you. My sister asked me, you know, you can get the pills online? At first you're wondering, is all this stuff legitimate? That was my first thought. Is this going to hurt me? Is this going to be real? Is this going to be something that's going to kill me? So what I did was I tried to vet it through Reddit. I took them and nothing ended up happening. I just didn't have the experience that I had read that other people were having: the nausea and vomiting and the passing blood clots and the bleeding -- that just wasn't happening to me. I was like, ''Well, I am so early on that maybe it did, and that's all it was.'' I had scheduled an appointment with my OB. I had told the nurse on the line I took \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. I told her the names of the two medications and I said, ''I'm not keeping the baby. I had done this. I wanted to see if it worked. I don't know if it did. I'm trying to make sure everything's OK, that I'm safe.'' She was like, ''Well, I'm going to let the provider know that you're wanting a scan, but I won't tell her about what you just told me.'' And I was like, ''Well, she needs to know.'' And she was like, ''Well, we're on a recorded line.'' And I just zipped up. I can't be truthful that this happened. I was terrified. I was dumbfounded. It was a very scary position to be in. The day of [the appointment], I was crying the entire time. I'm crying going in and crying while they're taking my blood pressure. I am crying the entire time they're doing the ultrasound. The father of the baby had come with me to be supportive. The hardest part was that she's talking to us like we just had an unplanned surprise pregnancy, but we're just scared right now, and everything's going to be fine. It's like, you have no idea what is going on. Like, not only do you not know we're not even in a relationship, but also I've already tried to do a medicated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that clearly failed. She was telling us clich\u233\'00\'E9 reassurances. Then she prints out the sonogram picture and hands him one and hands me one. And that made me want to die. Chelsea A college student in Tennessee, a state with a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in place. Because of complications with a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she had to get a later \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state. Before Dobbs, there was an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic on her block. I'm a full-time college student. I work as a waitress to support myself. I live in a onebedroom, with a cat. I barely afford my lifestyle as it is. I wouldn't be able to care for a child where I live. I'd have to move, I wouldn't have the money to do that, and I wouldn't be able to stay in school, I'd probably have to drop out. So it's just, like, no part of my life is ready for a child yet. I found this sticker in a bar bathroom. It was called AidAccess.org, and they'll mail you the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill for $150. I did the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at home. It was eight hours of extreme pain, and I had a lot of stuff come out of me. I thought, naturally, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  worked. I Googled it and then for two months it was, ''You'll still test positive for the pregnancy hormones.'' When I took a test six weeks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was still positive and it was like, ''Oh, that's normal. Take another one at some point.'' I was like, ''Oh, OK. I'm probably fine.'' It took me a while to register that I was pregnant again. And then when I go to the Planned Parenthood thinking it's just a different pregnancy, and they were like, ''Oh, you didn't feel the first trimester again because you were in the second trimester that whole time.'' They told me, ''Yeah, that's the same pregnancy.'' I don't have insurance. I'm in a college town. It's very hard to access any type of women's health care where I live. There was this clinic around the corner from me. I still pass by it. You would always see the people in their pink shirts in the back parking lot escorting people into the building, and then you would have protesters, like religious ones, right outside, protesting every day. And then the law passed. Now that building's shut down. Overgrown. I would have been able to walk on my way to class to this clinic and be like, ''Hey, I just want to check,'' and then I could have gotten taken care of right next to my house. Destini Spaeth A volunteer for the Prairie \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund in North Dakota, where she helps women figure out everything from insurance to gas money for access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. With volunteer-led organizations -- especially those navigating the post-Dobbs fallout, all the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans -- we don't have an off. We don't have an end of the day. All of this work needs to fit into our regular lives, on top of our jobs and our families. I answer emails in the middle of the night. I give callers my cellphone number so that they can text me if they have any questions or concerns. There seems to always be work to be done. There's always callers who are needing assistance. I shouldn't need to do this. I shouldn't need to be fielding 17 emails on a lunch break at my job to make sure that somebody gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I do dream of a time when this isn't required, and I can support people in other ways that isn't hustling money for them to pay for their appointments or finding them clinics. We're really at a combustion point of how much weight \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds are carrying, how desperate the need is, how cumbersome the landscape is. I never thought it could get this bad. Chelsea Souder A co-owner of Hope Clinic in Granite City, Ill. Because of its location and no-turnaway policy, the clinic is a last resort for many women who travel there from states that ban abortions. I hate to say it, but we're kind of living day by day, and we're navigating the system and cobbling things together from every corner we can across the country. But we're also absorbing a lot of costs. National cuts from the \uc2 National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Federation\uc2  came down late summer. We were part of another program that was a national funding system for a number of clinics in a few states that were receiving a lot of patients. That covered abortions in the first trimester 100 percent for people coming from banned states, and then it came to a close at the end of August. We completely lost that funding. We've worked really closely with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds to try to help fill some of the gaps, but the reality is, the gap is so massive. I'm talking about millions and millions of dollars in a year. In June of 2022 and thereafter was the most amount of money we've seen poured into this movement in a long time. But that money has slowed down across the board, for everybody, drastically. We have been trying to navigate an unsustainable system of people trying to essentially beg and crowdfund for their health care for a very long time. What we are seeing now is that system is buckling at the seams, because the need is bigger than anyone I think could have ever imagined. Interviews were edited for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241027ekar0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
