\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ballot Power of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Will Be Tested\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,221 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 They hope the issue helps their candidates. But some voters may support Republican candidates as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 With Election Day closing in, there are signs that a small but crucial segment of voters may back both \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and Republican candidates, a dynamic that could hurt Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in swing states and other Democratic candidates in pivotal races.\par \par \uc2 Ten states have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures on the ballot this year, and Democratic activists express confidence that most will pass, just as every similar state measure has won approval since Roe v. Wade was overturned in 2022.\par \par \uc2 But they remain less certain about how many of these newly engaged \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights voters will then support Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 ''The central question is, how much does \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  persuade people to vote for Democrats?'' said Angela Kuefler, a Democratic strategist working on several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures. ''The issue itself is still of utmost importance to people. It is still a big driver when it comes to their vote decision. The question is just the ceiling.''\par \par \uc2 In Arizona and Nevada, the presidential race is tight, but measures enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitutions are expected to cruise to victory. In Montana, Democrats are struggling to hold a Senate seat but expect to pass a similar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights referendum. And in Missouri, voters appear ready to back an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure while also re-electing Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , one of the strongest opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Congress.\par \par \uc2 The result could be a strange split screen: Voters act to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their states but also elect senators who have vocally supported restrictions, as well as former President Donald J. Trump, who has taken credit for overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 There's also the possibility that one or more of the measures could fail, an outcome that would be a first in the post-Roe environment.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe energized a new coalition of independents, liberals and some moderate Republicans behind Democratic candidates. Democrats embraced the issue as never before, marching to victories in midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Now, those new political tactics are being tested for the first time in the overwhelming heat of a presidential race.\par \par \uc2 There is little question that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights remains one of the strongest election issues for Democrats. A series of polls have shown more voters prefer Ms. Harris on the issue over Mr. Trump, helping drive a gender gap that is defining the race.\par \par \uc2 But for a small yet potentially decisive percentage of voters, supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights does not lead to checking the box for Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 About 12 percent of voters said they would vote for both Mr. Trump and an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights referendum, according to \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena College surveys in Arizona and Florida. While the polls found that a slight majority of these voters trust Mr. Trump to handle the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights more than Ms. Harris, most of them also do not rank the issue as a top priority.\par \par \uc2 ''If you are generally a pro-life Republican, who still wants some legal avenue for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you'll vote yes on that measure -- but you're still not a Democrat,'' said Barrett Marson, a Republican strategist in Arizona who is supporting Ms. Harris.\par \par \uc2 Trent Wichmann, 20, a student from Florida, said he planned to vote for the amendment because he believes a woman should be able to make her own decisions. But he also said he's also going to vote for Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''The main reason is his high focus on our borders. I think that is a huge problem,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are also facing an electorate that is not always well-informed.\par \par \uc2 In battleground states, one in five voters wrongly blame President Biden for ending the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though Mr. Trump was the one who appointed the three Supreme Court justices who helped overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump himself has struggled to settle on a clear position, equivocating before embracing the stance that the issue should be left to the states -- a message quickly adapted by Republicans in key races -- when he talks about the issue at all.\par \par \uc2 All the same, Republicans are breathing easier in swing states without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, Michigan voters approved a state constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also elected Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, largely because of her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 This year in Michigan, there is no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure. Republicans argue that allows voters to focus more on economic issues like inflation and high housing costs where polling indicates that their party has an edge.\par \par \uc2 ''People feel good about the fact that it's a done issue in Michigan,'' said Tudor Dixon, the Republican candidate who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape and incest and lost to Ms. Whitmer. ''It changes every race because now we can focus on issues that affect every family.''\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups prevailed in all seven \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures since Roe fell, they occurred during midterm and off-year elections.\par \par \uc2 In presidential elections, the electorate expands, making the political environment more unpredictable for candidates.\par \par \uc2 ''This is our first presidential year since the fall of Roe. It means that voters are turning out for a lot of other reasons beside the ballot measure,'' said Kelly Hall, executive director of the Fairness Project, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures in red and purple states. ''They may be making their partisan choices on immigration, on the economy, on infrastructure, on jobs.''\par \par \uc2 Some of the ballot initiatives themselves are also facing steeper challenges this year.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hall sees a path to victory in all five states where her group is working -- Arizona, Florida, Missouri, Montana and Nebraska -- but noted that the measures in Florida and Nebraska face significant opposition.\par \par \uc2 To pass, the Florida initiative requires support from more than 60 percent of voters, a threshold higher than any previous measure had to meet. While voters have turned out in more conservative states, including Kansas and Ohio, to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, they haven't exceeded 60 percent in any of those states.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, there will be two competing measures on the ballot -- a first since Roe was overturned. One would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state Constitution; the other would maintain the current ban on the procedure after 12 weeks, with limited exceptions. If voters pass both, the measure that wins the most support would be adopted in the state Constitution.\par \par \uc2 For their part, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists say that even if all the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures pass, their fight is far from over. They're looking ahead to challenging the referendums in court and feel they can advance their agenda if Mr. Trump wins.\par \par \uc2 And as they survey the political landscape, they are hopeful that the political focus of Democrats on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights may have hit a high-water mark.\par \par \uc2 ''This isn't something they will be able to do year after year,'' said Kristi Hamrick, strategist for Students for Life. And, she said, ''It will not be the end of what we can do state by state.''\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik and Kellen Browning contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik and Kellen Browning contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241027ekar0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
