\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Abortions Rise, Even in States With Rigid Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Cain Miller, Margot Sanger-Katz and Josh Katz \par \uc2 1,290 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new analysis shows how many women in states with bans are seeking procedures or pills from out-of-state providers.\par \par \uc2 In nearly every state that has banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the number of women receiving abortions increased between 2020 and the end of 2023, according to the most comprehensive account of all abortions by state since the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In the 13 states that enacted near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the number of women receiving abortions increased in all but three, according to the study. Some women traveled to clinics in states where abortions were legal. Others ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from U.S. doctors online, after doctors in other states started writing prescriptions under shield laws that protect them when they provide mail-order pills to patients in states with bans.\par \par \uc2 The only states with bans where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell during this period were Texas, where the decrease was small; Idaho, where it was larger; and Oklahoma, where the data showed an unusually large number of abortions in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, the study also found that abortions have continued to rise. There were roughly 587,000 abortions in the first half of this year, an increase of more than 12 percent from the same period in 2023.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a surprise to everyone,'' said David S. Cohen, co-author of the coming book ''After Dobbs: How the Supreme Court Ended Roe but Not \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 . ''I think most people thought there would be creativity and determination that would still get a lot of people abortions once Roe v. Wade was overturned. But I don't think anyone thought it would stay the same, let alone go up.''\par \par \uc2 Many state bans went into effect soon after June 2022, when the Supreme Court overturned Roe, which had guaranteed the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. Other states began putting in shield laws in the summer of 2023. The new data shows the extent to which these laws have blunted the effects of the bans.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have been arguing for a federal ban that would override state laws.\par \par \uc2 ''A serious breakdown in the law takes place when one state attempts to interfere with another state's efforts to protect their citizens,'' said \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , the president of Students for Life of America, a group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases could stall or reverse under a different presidential administration, or a major court decision. The Food and Drug Administration's embrace of telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  began in 2021 under the Biden administration. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has made protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a central theme of her campaign.\par \par \uc2 Former President Donald J. Trump has sent mixed messages about how he would regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, but has expressed openness to restricting access to them. A future F.D.A. could ban telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or reverse its approval of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills altogether. Federal officials could also impose restrictions on mailing any materials used in abortions. A lawsuit working its way through federal courts seeks similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The new analysis, published Tuesday by WeCount, a group overseen by academic researchers, includes counts of abortions from all known \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the United States since April 2022. It also uses clinic survey data from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group, on the number of women traveling between states for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and comparison data from 2020. Both organizations support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The numbers of abortions in ban states could be overstated. It was not widely possible to order pills under shield laws until the second half of last year, and the researchers' data on those orders was for that period. Their data on interstate travel, though, was for the full year, and they divided it by two. As a result, the number of women who traveled in the second half of the year may have been overestimated if fewer women traveled later in the year once they could order pills instead.\par \par \uc2 ''The people doing these studies are scientists doing the best they can with incomplete data,'' Professor Cohen said. ''It's a pretty good estimate, but they'll be the first to tell you there are still a lot of unknowns.''\par \par \uc2 For the first time, the report breaks out the number of abortions provided under the new shield laws. On average, it found, these laws are enabling 7,700 abortions a month in states with total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or six-week bans.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been illegal since July 2022, around 3,300 women got abortions in a six-month period last year, an increase of 16 percent from 2,850 in six months in 2020. Nearly half of women ordered pills under shield laws, and the rest traveled out of state. For women in West Virginia, abortions increased 57 percent, and in Tennessee, 38 percent.\par \par \uc2 Telehealth abortions were a big driver of the increases. Across all states, there were an average of 20,000 abortions per month in the period from April to June this year, up from about 8,000 per month in the same period last year. The number of in-person abortions was mostly stable, decreasing 3 percent during that time.\par \par \uc2 The expansion of telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been an important factor even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained legal, especially in rural areas. In Wyoming, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal but many women live far from clinics, telehealth accounted for 55 percent of abortions in the second quarter of this year, compared with 8 percent in New York.\par \par \uc2 Other factors have also contributed to the increase in abortions, researchers said. New clinics have opened, and a nationwide surge of publicity about the issue may have decreased stigma. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have helped people get access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  financially or logistically, enabling many women to travel out of state.\par \par \uc2 Eighty percent of the abortions counted by WeCount were reported directly by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or public health departments. The rest, in places that did not provide complete data, especially California, Florida, New Jersey and New York, were estimated based on other information.\par \par \uc2 These numbers do not include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills that were ordered from overseas pharmacies, or other abortions that did not involve a licensed U.S. clinician. The research does not measure whether every woman who orders \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills ends up taking them.\par \par \uc2 Comparisons to 2020 are not exact because the earlier data used slightly different methods. Over the last two years, some states have enacted new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, and some have reversed them. Florida's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban went into effect in May, and Iowa's six-week ban in July. The legal status of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also changed in Indiana, South Carolina and Wisconsin during the study period.\par \par \uc2 The bans have prevented some women who wanted abortions from getting them. They may not have been able to afford to travel, to find child care or take time off work. Some may have been uncomfortable ordering pills, or unaware of the options. Research suggests that these women tend to be teenagers; to be Black and Native American; and to have low incomes. Research on birth and death records from last year shows that the birthrate increased somewhat in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, as has infant mortality.\par \par \uc2 ''The data don't tell us anything about people wanting abortions and not getting them,'' said Dr. Alison Norris, a chair of WeCount and a professor of epidemiology at Ohio State.\par \par \uc2 Still, the data suggests that the Dobbs decision and the resulting bans were not effective in decreasing the overall prevalence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241023ekan0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
