\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Could Decide Races for State Justices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Wines \par \uc2 1,072 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Ohio, Arizona and elsewhere, progressive court candidates are hoping that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue that helped conservatives remake the federal judiciary will work for them this time.\par \par \uc2 The fight over the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has expanded into yet another corner of American political life: the races to fill seats on state supreme courts.\par \par \uc2 An effort is underway in Arizona to oust two justices who voted last spring to uphold a 160-year-old ban on most abortions.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, rulings by the state's highest state court to sharply limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have set off a campaign against three justices who are seeking re-election.\par \par \uc2 And in Montana, where new justices could undo a ruling that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access a constitutional right, outside money from both the left and right has turned contests for two court seats into multimillion-dollar elections.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  powered conservative efforts to remake the federal judiciary. This time, progressive candidates and their backers are betting that it can be the driving force in state court races -- but this time, to their benefit.\par \par \uc2 In 22 states, voters elect their supreme court justices. All but eight of them elect justices in contests that are officially nonpartisan, though the judges' political leanings are often quite apparent.\par \par \uc2 Spending on those races by partisan groups, including the national Republican and Democratic parties, is soaring.\par \par \uc2 State supreme court elections racked up a record $101 million in spending in the 2021-22 election cycle, including $45.7 million by outside interest groups, the Brennan Center for Justice has reported.\par \par \uc2 Those numbers now seem quaint. Last year, a race for one Wisconsin Supreme Court seat consumed $51 million, a national record. Republican gerrymandering of the State Legislature and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were prominent issues in that election, which flipped the court from a conservative majority to a liberal one.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ''not the only issue these courts are incredibly important for,'' said Deirdre Schifeling, the chief political and advocacy officer at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . But Ms. Schifeling said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ''top of mind'' for voters this year, and so the A.C.L.U. is spending $1.3 million in Montana on advertisements laying out the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views of candidates for state Supreme Court seats.\par \par \uc2 Fair Courts America, a national PAC tied to \uc2 Richard Uihlein\uc2 , a right-wing billionaire and founder of the Uline shipping supplies company, has given $400,000 so far to a local group backing the two Republican candidates in the Montana court contest.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Votes, the organization's independent advocacy arm, is spending $2.2 million in Montana on television and other advertising.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's ads in Montana and five other states are the organization's first in state supreme court contests. In past years, the group ''didn't have an immediate need to rely on state courts. We had Roe to fall back on,'' said Katie Rodihan, the director of state advocacy communications for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  Votes.\par \par \uc2 No more. In Montana and elsewhere, ''the state supreme court is really the front line for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In one sense, the emphasis on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflects the relentless pull of partisan politics on state courts, especially since a conservative \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  began offloading authority to the states over issues like redistricting, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gun rights.\par \par \uc2 But politics -- even \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics -- doesn't always hold sway. In Alabama, three justices who ignited a national debate by ruling that frozen embryos are children are seeking re-election unopposed.\par \par \uc2 And in nonpartisan court elections in 2022, voters in Kentucky, Montana and Arkansas rejected candidates with explicitly political messages and backed by outside money.\par \par \uc2 At least in nonpartisan races, ''voters are still able to express this idea that courts are not supposed to be partisan in the same way that other elected officers are,'' said Douglas Keith, the senior counsel in the judiciary program at the Brennan Center for Justice.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are spending money in response to the growing spending by Democrats. The national Republican Party's Judicial Fairness Initiative has said it will spend money in the same six states targeted by Democrats and progressives: Arizona, Michigan, Montana, North Carolina, Ohio and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Michigan and Ohio, the only two states where elections pose any likelihood of flipping a court's ideological majority, lead the pack.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hold a 4-to-3 majority on the Michigan court, where two seats are being contested.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and the A.C.L.U. are spending huge sums there on issue-oriented campaigns that are certain to favor the two Democrats who are running. An independent PAC calls them ''the only Supreme Court candidates who will protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Fair Courts America, meanwhile, has given $500,000 to a PAC run by the Ohio Chamber of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Democrats would have to win all three seats that are being contested to reverse the 4-3 Republican majority on the court, which took a turn to the right in elections in 2022. A Republican sweep, on the other hand, would give the party a 6-1 majority.\par \par \uc2 The outcomes of those contests will matter. Ohioans approved a constitutional amendment protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in 2023. Voters also are widely expected to approve another amendment in November that will require nonpartisan redistricting in a state Republicans have heavily gerrymandered.\par \par \uc2 Disputes over the details and the implementation of both amendments are likely to come before the state's next court, with huge political implications. Ads supporting Democratic candidates stress \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; Republican ads try to link the Democrats to national issues like immigration and crime.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and redistricting aren't the only issues tugging courts into the political arena. Voting rights, gun rights and L.G.B.T.Q. rights, all issues that resonate nationally, are also coming before state courts.\par \par \uc2 William K. Weisenberg, a prominent Ohio lawyer, is the co-chairman of an effort by the \uc2 American Bar Association\uc2  to restore confidence in American democracy and the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 ''The perception now is that for the candidates for the highest courts, it's not just their judicial philosophy, but their political affiliation that predicts how they will decide cases,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In the voting booth, Mr. Weisenberg said, ''most people don't pay attention. They just look at whether it's an R or D.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241017ekag00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
