\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Support Falls for Florida  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Effort\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,250 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Earlier surveys have shown higher support, but the state's Republican governor is working hard to defeat the initiative.\par \par \uc2 A ballot measure that would establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Florida Constitution is not on track to pass in November, according to a poll by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College.\par \par \uc2 A plurality of voters expressed support for the measure -- significantly more than those who said they would vote ''no'' -- and a large percentage of voters were undecided. But Florida requires 60 percent of voters to approve any ballot amendment, an unusually high threshold, and the poll found support for the measure falling well short of that.\par \par \uc2 Conducted between Sept. 29 and Oct. 6, the poll found that 46 percent of likely voters said they would vote for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment, 38 percent would vote against, and 16 percent said they did not know or refused to answer. Voters in the last category were twice as likely to support former President Donald J. Trump for president as they were to support Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have prevailed in all seven state ballot measures across the country in the two years since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are hoping to stop that momentum in Florida, where a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy took effect in May. It is one of 10 states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights questions on the ballot in November, but in eight of those states, the measures require only a simple majority to pass, and polling suggests that they will.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have prevailed even in states such as Kansas and Ohio that, like Florida, lean conservative, they have not topped 60 percent in any of those states. (The highest total was 58.97 percent in Kansas in 2022.)\par \par \uc2 Pollsters say it is hard to measure voter sentiment regarding ballot questions because the language of those questions often confuses voters.\par \par \uc2 Other polls on the Florida measure over the last two months have shown significantly more support. Two polls, conducted in August and September, showed more than a majority of voters supporting the measure -- 56 percent and 55 percent -- but still short of what is needed for it to pass. In another poll at the end of July, after Ms. Harris became the Democratic presidential nominee, 69 percent said they would vote yes. The Times/Siena poll, which was the highest quality survey of the state in months, also found higher support for Mr. Trump than other recent polls.\par \par \uc2 These earlier polls asked voters whether they intended to vote yes or no using the exact wording that appears on the ballot: ''No law shall prohibit, penalize, delay, or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability or when necessary to protect the patient's health, as determined by the patient's health care provider. This amendment does not change the Legislature's constitutional authority to require notification to a parent or guardian before a minor has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The Times used different, briefer language, asking likely voters whether they would vote yes or no on the amendment, ''that would if passed legalize abortions up to the point of fetal viability, or generally acknowledged to be the 24th week of pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 In polls in other states, voters were more likely to support questions that refer to ''viability'' than those that say ''24 weeks,'' a reflection of confusion surrounding pregnancy and the language of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The earlier polls were also conducted before the campaigns for and against the initiative began running ads, and before mail-in ballots began arriving at Florida homes.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican, has warned anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists that he believes the measure will succeed, and he has been raising money and leaning into the powers of his office to try to defeat it. Last week, his administration sent a letter to television stations threatening criminal action if they did not pull an ad supporting the ballot measure. The 30-second spot features a woman who terminated her second pregnancy before Florida's ban took effect, after she discovered that she had a large brain tumor.\par \par \uc2 ''If I did not end my pregnancy, I would lose my baby, I would lose my life and my daughter would lose her mom,'' the woman says in the ad. ''Florida has now banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in cases like mine.''\par \par \uc2 The DeSantis administration notes that the state's ban allows doctors to perform abortions when two doctors certify in writing that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to save the life of the pregnant woman. But doctors say they are reluctant to grant these exceptions because it is not clear what constitutes a life-threatening condition under the law, and any violations could result in prison sentences of up to five years. State data shows that out of 33,000 abortions in Florida this year, 86 were performed in cases of life-threatening emergencies, roughly one-third the number from last year.\par \par \uc2 The ballots that have begun appearing in mailboxes over the last couple of weeks also include a financial statement beneath the ballot question, which includes language that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups say is biased and speculative. That statement, which was approved by a Republican-controlled state panel, is more than twice as long as the ballot measure itself, and warns that the measure could result in high litigation costs for the state, as well as reduced revenues because it would result in fewer live births.\par \par \uc2 A website for the state agency that regulates health care providers says that the measure ''threatens women's safety,'' contrary to studies showing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safer than childbirth. And earlier, Mr. DeSantis sent police officers to the homes of voters who had signed petitions to place the measure on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 As in other polls on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the measure found the most support among women between the ages of 18 and 29, Black and Hispanic voters, and white voters with a college degree.\par \par \uc2 When asked which issue was most likely to decide their vote, the highest percentage of likely voters -- 30 percent -- said the economy but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was next on the list, tied with immigration at 12 percent. This is similar to other states, underscoring that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far more important to voters than it was before the court overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 A wide range of polls have found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is newly motivating Democratic voters since Roe's demise. That's a flip of the dynamic that had prevailed for years, when anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sentiment was more likely to drive Republican turnout.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are hoping that the ballot measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights might increase turnout for their candidates. But those since 2022 have attracted support from Republicans and outperformed some Democratic candidates even in reliably blue states like California.\par \par \uc2 Florida is not considered an electoral battleground in November. Still, some Democrats had held out a distant hope that the measure could help Ms. Harris and \uc2 Debbie Mucarsel-Powell\uc2 , the Democrat trying to unseat the Republican senator, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If it succeeds, the measure would have an enormous impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access not only in Florida but across the South. Until this year, Florida allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability, and it had become an alternative for women from states that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the court overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241010ekaa00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
