\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Georgia, New Energy From Harris Puts to Test An Electorate Still in Flux\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rick Rojas \par \uc2 1,324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many voters in the Atlanta suburbs were newly hopeful when Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  entered the race. But conservative values are still deeply rooted in the swing state.\par \par \uc2 A perpetually expanding sprawl encircles Atlanta, the capital of Georgia and arguably all of the South. Newcomers from all over have powered its rapid growth, lured by a sense of promise and possibility.\par \par \uc2 Because of the demographic shifts in these rapidly diversifying suburbs, Georgia recently emerged as a battleground state, after years of Republican dominance. And even though President Biden won the state in 2020, his prospects this year in Georgia were dim.\par \par \uc2 Then Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stepped up, and the energy shifted. Voters who struggled to feel enthusiastic about Mr. Biden were now excited and optimistic. The new dynamics may serve as the biggest test yet of Georgia's status as a swing state.\par \par \uc2 Over several weeks, we spoke to voters scattered across the constellation of suburban counties that make up the footprint of Metro Atlanta, finding a range of opinions as diverse as the area itself.\par \par \uc2 The prevailing issues echoed those expressed by voters virtually everywhere: the cost of living. Security at the southern border. Access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But voting rights, election integrity and the health of the country's institutions mattered here, as well. That was a direct result of 2020.\par \par \uc2 2020 Was a Lot\par \par \uc2 It was a year during which the country's fractured politics had increasingly given way to conflicting versions of reality. The pandemic was a public health crisis, or an economic one. \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protests were a necessary reckoning over institutional racism, or an explosion of violence and unrest that sullied cities like Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 That split-screen view was most evident with the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden had narrowly but legitimately won in Georgia, but former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and his associates were accused of engineering a plan to overturn it. Prosecutors in Atlanta argued that their actions broke the law, charging them in a racketeering case that is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Some followers of the former president believed his claim of a stolen victory. To others in Georgia, it looked like a campaign to diminish their voting rights.\par \par \uc2 The turmoil has caused fatigue.\par \par \uc2 Alena Scigliano has reacted by making a conscious choice to limit her exposure to the news.\par \par \uc2 ''Can I impact it? Can I have any control in it? If I can't do anything, it's not something I need to stress over,'' she said on an evening walk through her neighborhood in Fayette County, which went to Mr. Trump in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But as much as she has avoided talking politics with her neighbors, she said, she is not shy about her aversion to the former president.\par \par \uc2 ''I have deep, deep concerns about him being president again,'' said Ms. Scigliano, a therapist specializing in narcissistic abuse.\par \par \uc2 As Atlanta Grows, So Does the Cost of Living\par \par \uc2 For a long time, the pitch that hooked transplants from places like New York or California was simple: ''It's so cheap,'' said Heather Breslin, who moved to Atlanta two decades ago and works as a film producer. ''It's such an easy place to live.''\par \par \uc2 It's still less expensive than those coastal cities. But for some, it no longer feels like the steal it once did.\par \par \uc2 Zippora Fleming, 25, has a college degree and a job as a registered behavioral technician that can be enormously satisfying when the children she works with have breakthroughs. Just recently, a girl who was nonverbal started talking.\par \par \uc2 But she can't help but think that her generation has gotten a raw deal: The minimum wage is practically impossible to live on. A college education is not the ticket to prosperity that she thought it would be. She would love to have a place of her own, but rent prices are daunting.\par \par \uc2 ''I just want to live,'' she said. ''I just want to get out. I don't want to be stuck in my parents' house anymore.''\par \par \uc2 Chanel Kho, 45, has voted for Mr. Trump before and plans to do so again. She likes that he projects strength, she said, and wants to see him crack down on soaring prices -- and illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 ''He loves America and he loves the people and he cares about our freedom,'' said Ms. Kho, a patient care technician at a hospital, who emigrated from the Philippines in 2001.\par \par \uc2 Immigration: Opportunity or Displacement?\par \par \uc2 Cruise through the suburbs and you'll see plenty of \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 's and evangelical megachurches. But you'll also see Hindu temples and mosques, and a single strip mall with restaurants offering Peruvian, Colombian, Mexican, Japanese and Vietnamese fare.\par \par \uc2 The changing landscape makes the national debate over immigration policy feel deeply personal.\par \par \uc2 Arturo Foster was brought by his parents to the United States illegally as a child. He said he has worked since he was 13 and was forced out of his family home because of his sexual orientation. The Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals program helped him work his way toward citizenship and build a career and business of his own.\par \par \uc2 On the day we spoke, he had just gotten married to his partner, Chris Lipski.\par \par \uc2 He felt fortunate. He had found love and a sense of security, he said. Still, he feared for the people who were now in the position he had once been in. He worries the hostility directed at undocumented people had only intensified. The rhetoric, he said, had become dangerous.\par \par \uc2 ''It scares the hell out of me,'' he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rises as an Issue in the Suburbs\par \par \uc2 Even with Georgia's evolution -- it has elected Democrats for president and the Senate -- there are issues that reveal how conservative values are still deeply rooted in the state.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of them: The state has a ban on the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 While Republican elected officials in the state continue their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, recent polling shows that many women are motivated to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Ms. Harris returned to Georgia last week to give a speech highlighting the deaths of two women that she argued were the result of the state's strict limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm a childhood survivor of sexual abuse,'' said Robyn Rigs, 62, an independent voter who lives in Decatur. ''It's just important to me that it remains in our hands and that we get to make the decisions over our own bodies.''\par \par \uc2 The Harris Effect\par \par \uc2 Voters in Georgia could be forgiven for once again feeling as if Atlanta has become the center of the universe. It was the site of the disastrous debate performance that torpedoed Mr. Biden's re-election bid, and it was where Ms. Harris, just over a month later, made her first major campaign stop.\par \par \uc2 ''I see people with hope again,'' Ms. Rigs said. ''And I didn't see that two months ago.''\par \par \uc2 Part of that is because Ms. Harris has emerged as a more effective spokeswoman for some voters, particularly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But there was a measure of skepticism over her unusual path to the nomination, because she had avoided the test of a primary season. Others worried that both racism and sexism could be weaponized against her.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fleming, the registered behavioral technician, believed there was meaning beyond just symbolism in electing a Black woman like Ms. Harris. It would mean having a president, she said, who knew intimately the burden Black women so often face: The doctors who don't believe you when you say you're in pain. The assumptions made about your competence at work.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not saying she's our savior or anything,'' Ms. Fleming said. ''I like that I'm in the middle of history.''\par \par \uc2 Maya King contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Maya King contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241002eka200051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
