\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Vance Honed Debate Skills In Cauldron of Senate Race\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael C. Bender, McKinnon de Kuyper and Christina Kelso \par \uc2 1,949 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  assumed his first public office just last year. But to win that job, he had to survive about a dozen debates, sharpening his chops when facing opponents onstage.\par \par \uc2 Some politicians enjoy mixing it up with crowds on the campaign trail, while others revel in fund-raising.\par \par \uc2 JD Vance loves debates.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance, the first-term Republican senator from Ohio, will get his moment on the most prominent stage of his fledgling political career on Tuesday, when he represents Donald J. Trump's presidential ticket in a matchup against his Democratic rival, Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  of Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 It's a moment he has been anticipating for months.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance spoke eagerly about the vice-presidential debate shortly after being named to Mr. Trump's ticket in July. When President Biden dropped out of the race, Mr. Vance reacted with disappointment -- because now he would no longer debate Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Talk about take-backs,'' Mr. Vance said at his first solo campaign event. ''I was told I was going to get to debate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and now President Trump's going to get to debate her. I'm kind of pissed off about that if I'm being honest with you.''\par \par \uc2 For his face-off with Mr. Walz, Mr. Vance has been busy with debate prep, including semiregular ''murder board'' sessions in which his top advisers pummel him with pointed questions and heated attacks.\par \par \uc2 But his biggest advantage may be his recent experience. While Mr. Vance, 40, assumed his first public office just last year, to win that job he had to survive a gantlet of about a dozen primary and general-election debates.\par \par \uc2 A review of those events shows that Mr. Vance, a best-selling author with a rebellious streak, brings a similarly distinct style to his debates. He is aggressive and bold in his assertions. His pugnacity often leads to over-the-top claims, but he is also careful to present as more polite and thoughtful than the caricature of him portrayed by opponents.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at Mr. Vance's debating style -- and what it may tell us about his upcoming matchup against Mr. Walz.\par \par \uc2 He's quick on his feet.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance was a first-time candidate when he ran for the Senate in 2022, but no amount of experience could have prepared him for the extraordinary moment that unfolded in front of him as two Republican primary opponents nearly came to blows on a debate stage in Columbus, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Mike Gibbons, an investment banker, had mocked Josh Mandel, a former state treasurer, for having ''never spent a day in the private sector.'' Mr. Mandel, a veteran, stood up and pointed a finger back at Mr. Gibbons, saying, ''Two tours in Iraq -- don't tell me I haven't worked.''\par \par \uc2 They stood chest to chest, trading taunts as Mr. Vance watched wide-eyed. He laughed and reached for his microphone but reconsidered and placed it beside his lap.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance grew agitated as the confrontation continued. He leaned back in his chair. He examined the back of his tie. ''Goddamn, this is ridiculous,'' he blurted out as the moderator tried to restore order.\par \par \uc2 When it was his turn for a question a few minutes later, Mr. Vance, who, like Mr. Mandel, had served in the Marines, appeared ready to unload on Mr. Gibbons. Instead, Mr. Vance berated Mr. Mandel, who was ahead in the polls.\par \par \uc2 ''This guy wants to be a U.S. senator, and he's up here, 'Hold me back, hold me back, I got two tours in the Marine Corps,''' Mr. Vance said, using an exaggerated voice to mimic Mr. Mandel. ''What a joke. Answer the question, stop playing around.''\par \par \uc2 The moment was noted at Mar-a-Lago, where Mr. Trump would clinch the Senate race for Mr. Vance with an endorsement a few weeks later. It also showed Mr. Vance's ability to react quickly and not overthink the response to even the most unexpected of debate moments.\par \par \uc2 He has faced a folksy Democrat before.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance's opponent in the general election for the Senate seat two years ago, Tim Ryan, campaigned as a folksy, bipartisan Everyman similar to Mr. Walz.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ryan, a Democrat who at the time was an Ohio congressman, favored Cleveland sports jerseys on the trail and often stood behind the debate lectern with his hand tucked casually into the pocket of his suit pants as he leveled attacks at Mr. Vance while referring to him as ''buddy.''\par \par \uc2 ''My turn, pal -- my turn,'' Mr. Ryan said, holding up his open palm as Mr. Vance tried to interrupt during one of their debates.\par \par \uc2 ''Oh, buddy, your turn,'' a frustrated Mr. Vance shot back while waving off Mr. Ryan with his hand.\par \par \uc2 In one attack, Mr. Ryan referred to old neighborhood friends as a way to attack Mr. Vance as too much of a supplicant to Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know anybody I grew up with, I don't know anybody I went to high school with that would allow somebody to take their dignity like that,'' Mr. Ryan said.\par \par \uc2 To better prepare for Mr. Walz's Minnesota version of ''Midwestern nice,'' the Vance team recruited Representative \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  to play the role of the governor in a mock debate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Emmer is a fellow Minnesotan with a similarly reliable love of sports -- Mr. Walz coached high school football, while Mr. Emmer played college hockey before coaching youth teams. Mr. Emmer is deeply familiar with Mr. Walz after having served with him for several terms in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Before his mock debate with Mr. Vance on Friday in Cincinnati, Mr. Emmer studied for the role and even held a mock debate of the mock debate.\par \par \uc2 ''He's going to do a great job,'' Mr. Emmer said on Sunday on ABC. ''Once he understands that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is just going to try and deflect and go into this folksy whatever, he'll hold him accountable.''\par \par \uc2 There is almost no question he hasn't heard.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has had a strong sense of self-confidence when speaking to reporters since his days working in media relations for the Marines. ''The experience taught me a valuable lesson: that I could do it,'' Mr. Vance wrote in his memoir, ''Hillbilly Elegy.'' ''I could work 20-hour days when I had to. I could speak clearly and confidently with TV cameras shoved in my face.''\par \par \uc2 He has brought that assurance to the campaign trail, where he has been interviewed dozens of times on cable networks and Sunday morning news broadcasts, and by local TV, radio and print reporters. An unusual twist during his campaign rallies is a regular Q. and A. session with reporters.\par \par \uc2 Those interactions have also doubled as debate prep. Mr. Vance's team says there is probably no issue he'll be asked about on Tuesday that he hasn't already fielded.\par \par \uc2 A key has been his willingness to wade far beyond the friendly terrain of conservative news media and to face more confrontational questions. Those appearances have helped him sharpen his skills.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance can laugh off questions, quickly pivot to turn almost any issue into an attack on Ms. Harris and rarely strays far from the campaign message. His interview with Dana Bash that aired two weeks ago on CNN showed he could get frustrated in the moment but also quickly recover.\par \par \uc2 In that interview, Mr. Vance defended his baseless claim that Haitian migrants were eating cats by saying that he was willing to ''create stories'' to get the news media focused on issues. Ms. Bash seized on those words.\par \par \uc2 ''So, the eating dogs and cats thing is not accurate?'' she asked.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance tilted his head and winced. The damage had been done, but Mr. Vance tried to clarify and dig himself out.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Dana\uc2 , it comes from firsthand accounts from my constituents,'' he said. ''I say that we're creating a story, meaning we're creating the American media focusing on it.''\par \par \uc2 Afterward, Democrats cheered the interview as exposing Mr. Vance's duplicity. Conservatives, however, applauded Mr. Vance for aggressively pushing back on Ms. Bash.\par \par \uc2 He still hasn't quite squared his position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance's 2022 debates generated headlines when he supported a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and again when he said he supported exceptions but refused to name them.\par \par \uc2 Now he's running on a ticket with Mr. Trump, who has tried to avoid questions about a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by saying that it was a question for the states.\par \par \uc2 The issue has created some awkward moments on the trail. Mr. Vance has resorted to answering the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  questions by subtly distancing himself from the top of the ticket, framing his response as ''President Trump's position'' or ''President Trump's view.''\par \par \uc2 Even when asked to articulate his own position, he pivots.\par \par \uc2 ''What do you want to see happen with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?'' he was asked in DePere, Wis.\par \par \uc2 ''Well,'' Mr. Vance said, before adding, ''this is President Trump's view, to be clear: Each state ought to make their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.''\par \par \uc2 Further complicating the issue was a public correction from Mr. Trump, who said that he never told Mr. Vance that he would veto a national ban.\par \par \uc2 When asked about the veto, Mr. Vance parroted Mr. Trump's answer that it was a meaningless question because a ban was unlikely to pass a divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 ''Let's not engage in stupid hypotheticals that are never going to happen,'' Mr. Vance said in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 But engaging in hypotheticals seems to be exactly what Mr. Vance has done -- he has been a vocal supporter for a national ban proposed last year by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 He has some Trumpy moves.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance has several moves when it comes to political communications.\par \par \uc2 He has few qualms about sanding down facts to sharpen his attacks on an opponent, but he is careful not to appear out of control in front of a camera. He's a policy wonk who was once a visiting scholar at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , a conservative think tank. But he has also perfected one of Mr. Trump's go-to moves -- turning the tables and accusing his opponents of doing whatever it is they're accusing him of doing.\par \par \uc2 In the 2022 debate with Mr. Ryan, Mr. Vance illustrated the technique by trying to boomerang an attack from Mr. Ryan back at him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ryan accused Mr. Vance of peddling the ''great replacement theory,'' the belief that Western elites want to ''replace'' and marginalize white Americans.\par \par \uc2 As Mr. Ryan spoke, Mr. Vance -- whose wife, Usha Chilukuri, is the daughter of Indian immigrants -- swayed behind a lectern awaiting his turn to speak. He fidgeted, moving his hands from the sides of the lectern before folding them in front of him.\par \par \uc2 Finally, he had enough.\par \par \uc2 ''So,'' Mr. Vance said, holding up his hand as if trying to stop Mr. Ryan.\par \par \uc2 ''Hold on, JD, stop interrupting,'' Mr. Ryan said.\par \par \uc2 ''No, this is disgusting,'' Mr. Vance shot back. ''Because here's exactly what happens when the media and people like Tim Ryan accuse me of engaging in the great replacement theory.\par \par \uc2 ''I'll tell you exactly what happens, Tim,'' he continued. ''What happens is that my own children -- my biracial children -- get attacked by scumbags online and in person because you are so desperate for political power that you'll accuse me, the father of three beautiful biracial babies, of engaging in racism. We are sick of it.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241001eka100053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
