\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Republican Candidates Keep Straying Into a Dangerous Area:  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a campaign they would like to center on the economy and the border, Republican candidates keep drifting back to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, an issue that favors Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Republicans don't want to touch \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , their most troublesome issue since the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But like a toothache they keep probing with their tongue, they just can't seem to help it.\par \par \uc2 This week, it was Bernie Moreno, the Republican running to unseat Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  in Ohio, suggesting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights shouldn't concern women over the age of 50 -- ''I don't think that's an issue for you,'' he said on tape in Warren County, Ohio, over the weekend, as first reported by the NBC affiliate in Columbus.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, in Pennsylvania, former President Donald J. Trump simply declared that women won't ''be thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' once he's elected.\par \par \uc2 And before his porn website revelations, Lt. Gov. Mark Robinson of North Carolina, the Republican candidate running to be the state's governor, was seen in a newly resurfaced video from 2022 waving his hand in front of his groin, advising women seeking birth control that they just need to ''get this under control.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been eager to campaign on a southwestern border that at times has seemed uncontrolled, and on the lingering sting of inflation, both issues where voters continue to prefer Mr. Trump. But the one issue surveys have shown the party should be steering clear of is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the most recent \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena College poll, 54 percent of registered voters trusted Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on the issue, against 39 percent who trusted Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have fared well in virtually every election since 2022, when the Supreme Court repealed the constitutional protections enshrined in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Yet in competitive contests, Republicans keep bringing it up, and in terms that their opponents have argued are disrespectful, if not contemptuous, of women. Even \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , the former Republican governor of South Carolina who challenged Mr. Trump for the nomination, felt compelled to weigh in on Mr. Moreno's comments, posing the question on social media, ''Are you trying to lose the election? Asking for a friend.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Moreno, running in a state where Mr. Trump holds a clear lead but he has struggled, was caught on tape not only stoking the fire around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also perhaps rekindling the flame that Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  of Ohio, the Republican vice-presidential nominee, had set alight with his resurfaced ''childless cat lady'' comments.\par \par \uc2 ''Sadly, there's a lot of suburban women, a lot of suburban women that are like, listen, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is it,'' Mr. Moreno said at a Warren County, Ohio, town hall. ''If I can't have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this country whenever I want, I will vote for anybody else.''\par \par \uc2 He went on, ''it's a little crazy, by the way, especially for women who are, like, past 50. I'm thinking to myself, 'I don't think that's an issue for you.'''\par \par \uc2 Last year, 57 percent of Ohio voters cast ballots to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's constitution. Mr. Brown quickly seized on his comments, circulating the video on social media.\par \par \uc2 A Moreno campaign spokeswoman, Reagan McCarthy, said the comments were clearly ''a tongue-in-cheek joke about how \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  and members of the left-wing media like to pretend that the only issue that matters to women voters is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 ''Bernie's view is that women voters care just as much about the economy, rising prices, crime, and our open southern border as male voters do,'' she continued, ''and it's disgusting that Democrats and their friends in the left-wing media constantly treat all women as if they're automatically single-issue voters on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brown shot back in a statement, ''As a man over the age of 50, I care deeply about a woman's right to make health care decisions for herself -- for my daughters, my granddaughters, and all Ohio women, regardless of their age.''\par \par \uc2 Of course, nobody can touch Mr. Trump for digressions, especially those that carry him into politically fraught territory. Polls have shown a vast gender gap that favors Ms. Harris, and in his effort to woo back some women -- including the ''suburban women'' mocked by Mr. Moreno who view \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a top issue -- the former president has made a number of assertions about life under another Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, at a speech on economic policy, he showed he is still smarting from the backlash that has come since his nominees to the Supreme Court tipped the balance on the repeal of Roe v. Wade. He went into an extended riff on how women will no longer be fearful, sad, depressed and otherwise demoralized if he prevails in November.\par \par \uc2 He concluded, ''Women will be happy, healthy, confident and free,'' and ''you will no longer be thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Asked what Mr. Trump meant by that, his campaign responded by reiterating his opposition to a ''federal ban'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and attacking Ms. Harris for supporting taxpayer-funded ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up until birth,'' something she does not support.\par \par \uc2 Other comments from Republicans on top of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  have come in newly resurfaced videos. Mr. Robinson's hand gesture dates back to January 2022 when the lieutenant governor of North Carolina appeared at Hilltop Baptist Church in Thomasville, N.C., to decry programs that teach sexual education and provide birth control. Instead, Mr. Robinson promoted abstinence until marriage.\par \par \uc2 ''We love to tell our young women that we want to empower you,'' he said, offering his own ''novel idea'' for getting female lives in check. ''Why don't you use some of that building up of your mind and building up of empowerment to move down here, to this region down here,'' he said, waving his hand near his groin. ''Get this under control.''\par \par \uc2 To be sure, it has been difficult for Republican candidates to avoid the issue entirely, in part because legalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the ballot in several states, including Arizona and Nevada, both swing states, and Montana, which has a Senate election that could tip the balance of power in the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Sam Brown, a combat veteran grievously wounded in Afghanistan, would like his remarkable biography, not his past statements opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to be the focus of his challenge to Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent in Nevada. But Mr. Brown was caught on a recording obtained by The Nevada Independent late last month saying in private what he has been less than clear about in public -- that he opposes the Nevada ballot measure to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal until fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not for changing our existing law,'' Mr. Brown said at a campaign event in Las Vegas. ''Our existing law has been in place for over 34 years. The ballot measure would change the law and essentially have no limit on access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' (In fact, the measure would place limits after viability, around the 24th week of pregnancy.)\par \par \uc2 The Rosen campaign quickly ran an attack ad framing Mr. Brown as a ''MAGA extremist trying to take away \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.''\par \par \uc2 In response to Democratic efforts to capitalize, Mr. Brown made a lengthy video trying to explain his position because, he complained, ''It's the only issue \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  wants to talk about.''\par \par \uc2 He stressed that Nevada voters could decide on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws of the state and promised never to ''vote for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.''\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Mr. Brown's communications director, Raegan Lehman, added, ''To no one's surprise, the Democrats continue to lie about Sam's common-sense position, because they refuse to talk about the abysmal failure that is \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 's record, that's driven up costs on housing, groceries and energy for Nevada families.''\par \par \uc2 Even when Republicans have tried to take Mr. Trump's position that he will leave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions to the states, they have at least allowed Democrats to re-inject \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the campaign conversation. Tim Sheehy, the businessman and military veteran with a strong shot of unseating Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  in Montana, was trying to take the Trump line late last month when he said he respected California's right to have lenient \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 ''If California says, you know, we're going to fund abortions with California taxpayer dollars all day long, I think it's terrible and disgusting, but if that's what the California voters want to do with their state, OK,'' he said at a Republican women's open house.\par \par \uc2 Democrats latched onto the tone, not the policy, quoting over and over that he also said, ''I find \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a terrible, terrible thing. I want it all to end tomorrow.''\par \par \uc2 The Sheehy campaign did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240927ek9r0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
