\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ohio Republican Senate Candidate Faces Fire Over Comments About Suburban Women\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chris Cameron \par \uc2 426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mr. Moreno, a Republican running for Senate in Ohio, suggested that older women shouldn't care about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they were too old to have children, comments \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  pointedly criticized.\par \par \uc2 Bernie Moreno, the Republican Senate candidate in Ohio, is facing criticism over demeaning remarks he made last week about women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including from \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , the former Republican presidential candidate and one of the most prominent women in her party.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on Friday at a town hall in Warren County, Ohio, Mr. Moreno characterized many suburban women as ''single-issue voters'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, suggesting that older women should not care about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they were too old to have children.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a little crazy, by the way -- especially for women that are like past 50,'' Mr. Moreno said, drawing laughter from the crowd. ''I'm thinking to myself: I don't think that's an issue for you.''\par \par \uc2 In a social media post on Tuesday morning quoting Mr. Moreno's remarks, Ms. Haley, the former governor of South Carolina, addressed the Senate candidate: ''Are you trying to lose the election? Asking for a friend.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Haley, who was former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's top rival in the Republican presidential primaries this year, has endorsed his candidacy even as she has offered advice and criticism to him and the party from the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 In interviews on Fox News, Ms. Haley has said that the party needs a ''serious shift'' to defeat Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , saying this month that Mr. Trump and his running mate, Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  of Ohio, ''need to change the way they speak about women.''\par \par \uc2 ''You don't need to call Kamala dumb,'' Ms. Haley said, adding that ''she didn't get this far, you know, just by accident'' and that ''she's a prosecutor. You don't need to go and talk about intelligence or looks or anything else.''\par \par \uc2 She added that ''when you call even a Democrat woman dumb, Republican women get their backs up, too.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats have embraced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as an issue that they see as advantageous to them, spotlighting Mr. Trump's bragging about appointing three of the Supreme Court justices who voted to end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enshrined in Roe v. Wade, and pinning their hopes of winning control of the Senate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiatives. Voters, by a wide margin, say they trust Ms. Harris to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240926ek9q0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
