\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Most Voters in Arizona Back Establishing Right To  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Poll Finds\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Igielnik \par \uc2 754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A ballot measure codifying ''the fundamental right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' is supported by 58 percent of the state's likely voters, according to a New York Times/Siena College poll.\par \par \uc2 A majority of likely voters in Arizona said they planned to support an amendment to the state's Constitution codifying ''the fundamental right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' according to the latest poll from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College.\par \par \uc2 The poll found that 58 percent of likely voters were in favor of the amendment, putting support above the 50 percent threshold needed to pass. The poll also found former President Donald J. Trump leading Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in the state, 50 percent to 45 percent.\par \par \uc2 The amendment, Proposition 139, would establish a fundamental right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prohibit the state from restricting or banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability -- the point at which a fetus can survive outside the uterus, generally around 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A little over one-third of voters in the state said they intended to vote no on the amendment.\par \par \uc2 The results are in line with recent polling in the state suggesting the ballot measure is likely to pass the threshold for support.\par \par \uc2 The amendment's supporters see it as an important step to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Opponents say it goes too far by allowing abortions up until around 24 weeks of pregnancy. Arizona law currently allows abortions up until 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Majorities of voters across every demographic group said they intended to support the measure. Younger voters and college-educated voters were especially likely to be in favor of it.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, more men than women planned to support the measure, 62 percent to 54 percent. Young men were especially likely to support the amendment.\par \par \uc2 ''Being a man, I don't have a dog in the fight necessarily, but I think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is about taking care of people's well-being,'' said Gage Billick, 32, of Phoenix. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is important health care.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Billick says he plans to vote in support of the measure and was motivated by the move by Arizona's highest court this summer to reinstate an 1864 law that banned nearly all abortions. ''That law was written before Arizona was even a state,'' Mr. Billick said. ''It was pretty shocking and un-American.''\par \par \uc2 The poll found some degree of bipartisan support. About 30 percent of Republicans said they would vote yes, along with a vast majority of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Still, support for the amendment fell short of the 66 percent of Arizona voters who said they wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be always or mostly legal.\par \par \uc2 These voters, who said they wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal but planned to vote no, were more likely to be Republicans and Trump supporters. They were also more likely to be white voters without a college degree who support some access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain legal, particularly in cases where the health of the mother is concerned,'' said Diane Fitzsimmons, 57, a real estate agent in Apache Junction. ''I was OK with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first trimester, but this bill goes too far into pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 Overall, 18 percent of voters in the state say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the most important issue to them, a higher share than in Georgia or North Carolina. An overwhelming majority of voters who cited it as their most important issue said they thought Ms. Harris would do a better job handling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Mr. Trump would.\par \par \uc2 The economy remains issue No. 1 for the largest share of voters in Arizona: About 30 percent said inflation or the economy was central to their vote. And Mr. Trump holds a wide advantage with those voters.\par \par \uc2 The amendment, which is one of several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot initiatives across the country this fall, is seen by Democrats as a potential opportunity to increase turnout, since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come to be a motivating issue on the left.\par \par \uc2 Polling on ballot initiatives is notoriously challenging. Ballot language can be complicated for voters to understand to begin with, and they do not always understand which side accurately represents their views. Translating ballot language into a clear polling question adds another degree of complexity.\par \par \uc2 The group Arizona for \uc2  Access submitted signatures in Phoenix in July for a ballot measure to enshrine \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARK HENLE/THE ARIZONA REPUBLIC, VIA USA TODAY NETWORK) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240924ek9o0004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
