\par\par \uc2 POLITICAL MEMO\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Use New Fuel to Cast Opponents as 'Team Misogyny'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,096 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 26\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The issue of gender dominated the campaign over a week that included a scandal in North Carolina and reporting on the fatal fallout of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 In a race between a Democrat who could be America's first female president and a Republican who has been found liable for sexual abuse, the issue of gender was always going to be inescapable.\par \par \uc2 But this week, the subject surged to the forefront of the fall contest in new and vivid ways, as Democrats found fresh fuel for their argument that today's Republican Party is disrespectful of women and their autonomy -- sometimes with dangerous consequences.\par \par \uc2 On Monday and Wednesday, the deaths of two mothers in Georgia were linked to the state's far-reaching \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in new reports from \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the deeply conservative Republican nominee for governor in North Carolina scrambled to deny that he had made graphic and incendiary remarks on a pornographic forum, including about women.\par \par \uc2 And on Friday, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  took to a stage in Atlanta to argue with new urgency that the Republican Party was infringing on some of the most personal decisions a woman can make.\par \par \uc2 ''It's clear that they just don't trust women,'' said Ms. Harris, speaking a day after joining a livestreamed event with \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  that attracted hundreds of thousands of viewers. ''Well, we trust women.''\par \par \uc2 And on Friday night, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  claimed on Truth Social, in all capital letters, that women were poorer, less healthy, less safe on the streets, and ''MORE DEPRESSED AND UNHAPPY THAN THEY WERE FOUR YEARS AGO.'' Mr. Trump, who has taken credit for helping overturn Roe v. Wade, added that ''YOU WILL NO LONGER BE THINKING ABOUT \b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 , BECAUSE IT IS NOW WHERE IT ALWAYS HAD TO BE, WITH THE STATES.''\par \par \uc2 For years, Democrats have tried to paint their Republican opponents as anti-woman, with mixed results.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, they effectively highlighted Republican comments, like the use of the term ''legitimate rape'' by Todd Akin, a Senate candidate in Missouri, to press their claims of a G.O.P. ''war on women.''\par \par \uc2 Four years later, however, Mr. Trump defeated another would-be first female president, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , despite being caught on a recording bragging about sexually assaulting women. And in plenty of races before and after, many Americans simply did not buy the Democratic argument that Republicans would take away \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights if given the chance.\par \par \uc2 But after Mr. Trump's Supreme Court nominees helped overturn Roe v. Wade in 2022, millions of Americans suddenly found themselves voting to protect or restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access -- and to punish Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Now, in an unpredictable presidential election shaped by a yawning gender gap and simmering concerns about the economy, Democrats hope \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will be galvanizing once again. Increasingly, they are drawing voters' attention to the cascade of disturbing stories tied to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that many Americans view as draconian.\par \par \uc2 ''Every week, there are different examples of the harmful consequences of these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans,'' said Molly Murphy, a pollster for the Harris campaign. ''Being able to elevate and show that this has real consequences to real people is an important part of our campaign.''\par \par \uc2 That was a goal of Ms. Harris's appearance in Atlanta. She and other Democrats are casting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a health care matter that should be nonpartisan and that carries vast implications for a broad swath of Americans.\par \par \uc2 And when Democrats highlight Republicans' past comments calling for strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, they hope that voters will now take those candidates at their word.\par \par \uc2 That is why, despite the near-unprintable statements reportedly made by Lt. Gov. Mark Robinson of North Carolina that CNN uncovered this week, some national Democrats are using the scandal to emphasize his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, rather than focusing on the most salacious comments.\par \par \uc2 A television ad in North Carolina, announced on Friday by the Harris campaign, called ''Both Wrong,'' showcased Mr. Trump's past praise of Mr. Robinson, alternating with video clips of the North Carolinian discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ad shows Mr. Robinson declaring that abortions amount to ''killing a child because you weren't responsible enough to keep your skirt down.''\par \par \uc2 ''This is 'War on Women' on steroids,'' said former Representative \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2 , a Virginia Republican who is voting this year for Ms. Harris, the first Democratic presidential candidate to ever earn her vote, she said.\par \par \uc2 Compared with Mr. Akin's remarks, Ms. Comstock said, this moment is ''exponentially politically toxic because there's nothing worse than being a hypocrite, particularly on these things that are so toxic with women.''\par \par \uc2 ''It is Team Misogyny with Trump,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Penny Nance, who leads the socially conservative group Concerned Women for America, suggested that it was unfair to link the latest reported comments about Mr. Robinson to Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''If this is confirmed as true, then he should drop out of the race,'' she said of Mr. Robinson, adding: ''That's not President Trump's fault. I mean, give me a break.''\par \par \uc2 Karoline Leavitt, a spokeswoman for Mr. Trump, said in a statement that he was ''loved by millions of women across the country'' and would prioritize ''expanding access to child care and paid family leave'' in a future administration. She also sought to distance him from the idea of a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the United States.\par \par \uc2 ''He will not sign a federal ban when he is back in the White House,'' Ms. Leavitt said. ''President Trump also supports universal access to contraception and I.V.F.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans have plenty of advantages in this race: For many voters, Mr. Trump is seen as the change candidate. In polling, he has the edge on the economy, a top issue for many Americans. And while Ms. Harris has benefited from a surge in support from women, Mr. Trump has strong standing among male voters.\par \par \uc2 But there is a major difference between this election and all the times before when Democrats cast their Republican rivals as anti-woman.\par \par \uc2 ''Back then, that's what the Democrats had: They had outrageous statements'' from their opponents to discuss, said Christine Matthews, a pollster who has worked with Republicans but opposes Mr. Trump, citing the 2012 races.\par \par \uc2 ''Now,'' she said, ''they can point to policies.''\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris said of Republicans that ''it's clear that they just don't trust women.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY AUDRA MELTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A26. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240922ek9m0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\p