\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 States to Vote on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights and Limits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allison McCann and Amy Schoenfeld Walker \par \uc2 641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Voters in a record 10 states will decide whether to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state constitutions this fall. In Nebraska, voters face two measures: one favoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and another that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights hope to continue their winning streak in both blue and red states, after a successful run of measures since the overturn of Roe v. Wade. Opponents have been working to keep these measures off the ballot or make them hard to pass.\par \par \uc2 How the measures would amend constitutions\par \par \uc2 Of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures, most would recreate the standard set by Roe, which protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until ''viability'' -- the point at which a fetus could survive outside the uterus, or around 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, where two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures take opposite stances, state officials have said that the measure with the most supportive votes would take effect.\par \par \uc2 The New York proposal does not explicitly mention \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but proponents say it would prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions by stopping discrimination based on pregnancy outcomes or \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  choices.\par \par \uc2 How the measures could change the landscape\par \par \uc2 Twenty-two states currently ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the procedure is broadly legal in the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 If every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure is successful this fall, five states would see big changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the months after Election Day. Arizona, Florida, Missouri, Nebraska and South Dakota have existing bans and restrictions, and the ballot measures would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly legal again.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota's amendment would prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions only in the first trimester, and the State Legislature is likely to pass new restrictions after that point.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado, Maryland, Montana and New York, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already legal and winning measures would enshrine its status. Colorado's measure would further expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access by allowing the use of public funds to pay for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 If all of the measures fail, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in each state would be unchanged -- at least for now.\par \par \uc2 If the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure in Nebraska passes, it would enshrine restrictions after the first trimester, which is similar to the state's current law.\par \par \uc2 What the polls say\par \par \uc2 A majority of U.S. adults say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, according to recent national polling from the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 . State-level polling of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attitudes is rarer, but a 2023 survey from the Public Religion Research Institute showed majority support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in every state where the issue will be on the ballot this fall, except for South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Polls that ask specifically about this year's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures show broad support for the proposals, but experts say such polls should be interpreted with caution. Ballot measure language can be complex and confusing for the public and therefore difficult to poll. In addition, many of these polls are run by partisan groups that may have an agenda.\par \par \uc2 Still, polls from varied organizations reveal that a majority of people would support the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measures on the ballot in Arizona, Missouri, New York and South Dakota. But in Florida, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure must reach 60 percent support to pass, some polls have shown it falling short of that threshold.\par \par \uc2 Where voters have backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 Since Roe was overturned in 2022, every ballot measure to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has passed and every measure to restrict or weaken those rights has failed.\par \par \uc2 The winning measures cemented \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in California, Michigan and Vermont, and tossed out restrictions on the procedure in Ohio. Voters in Kansas, Kentucky and Montana rejected efforts to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik and Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240921ek9l0004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
