\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is a Winning Issue for Harris\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cecile Richards \par \uc2 1,332 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Among the many critical issues at stake in the 2024 election, one will be central for many Americans: Whom do you trust to make medical decisions -- women and their doctors, or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and JD Vance?\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  cast the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stark relief in her first debate with former President Trump last week, striking a chord with voters across political lines. Ms. Harris's answers on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged as her strongest moments onstage in a strong night for her overall -- and provided a glimpse of a winning strategy for this election.\par \par \uc2 That involves the Harris-Walz ticket turning the volume up to 11 on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ms. Harris, Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and their campaign surrogates must keep emphasizing -- on the stump, in ads and at every chance they get -- how Mr. Trump and Mr. Vance are impossible to trust on these issues.\par \par \uc2 They must refuse to let Mr. Trump and other Republicans suggest that leaving decisions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the states is a benign proposition and continue to point to the wide-ranging impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on pregnancy care, miscarriage treatment and training opportunities for an entire generation of doctors.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris would do well, even, to devote an entire speech to the issue, laying out her plan to take action in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with or without Congress.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, she should keep deploying campaign surrogates such as Kaitlyn Joshua, a mother from Baton Rouge, La., who described being denied care at not one but two emergency rooms in the midst of a painful miscarriage because of her state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Personal stories like these can break through and reach voters more effectively than any campaign talking point. According to PerryUndem, a research and polling firm, exposure to these stories is helping to shift public opinion in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- an imperative in this election and beyond.\par \par \uc2 As my mother, Ann Richards, a former governor of Texas, liked to say, it's only when you're getting sick of saying something that people really start to hear it. And Americans need to hear this message.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Ms. Harris has become one of the Democratic Party's most skillful communicators on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She doesn't shy away from using the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' or trot out tired political rhetoric. She talks about these issues the way millions of Americans understand them: in terms of freedom, health and family.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Ms. Harris is believed to have been the first sitting vice president or president to visit a health center that provides abortions, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in St. Paul, Minn. With her were some of Minnesota's fiercest champions for reproductive freedom, one of whom seems to have made an impression: Mr. Walz.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris understands that our country will never reach its full potential as long as half of its citizens are denied the most basic right to make their own decisions about their lives and futures. Simply put: She gets it.\par \par \uc2 Compare that to Mr. Trump, who for months has struggled to walk a fine line: convincing voters he's actually not so bad on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while signaling to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that he'll deliver for them again as president. When asked whether he would sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, his answers are unintelligible. He bends over backward to distance himself from Project 2025, a 900-plus-page plan written by some of his closest allies filled with deeply unpopular proposals, including effectively banning medication abortions nationwide and forcing states to monitor pregnancies. Scrambling to make up lost ground after Alabama's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban led the State Supreme Court to temporarily halt fertility care, he has claimed to be ''a leader in fertilization'' and promised to pay for Americans' in vitro fertilization treatments.\par \par \uc2 As tempting as it is to dismiss the blatant lies and desperate pandering, this strategy has worked for Mr. Trump before. That's why it's so important between now and November for Ms. Harris to relentlessly remind voters of Mr. Trump's past actions and hold him accountable for the suffering caused by overturning Roe. And she should keep relying on specific, real-world examples, such as the story of a 12-year-old in Mississippi who was raped by a stranger in her yard. Because of her state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, the girl started seventh grade as the parent of a newborn.\par \par \uc2 For decades, conventional political wisdom within certain Democratic circles went something like this: It's all very well to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York and California, but that will never fly with voters in the South or the Midwest. Ms. Harris has wisely rejected that premise. And for the next seven weeks, she must push as hard as she can.\par \par \uc2 I've seen firsthand why addressing this issue head-on matters. When I was president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , one of the first big fights in my time with the organization happened after the South Dakota legislature passed a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The local \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  chief executive at the time, Sarah Stoesz, suggested we take the matter directly to the voters.\par \par \uc2 I'll never forget the campaign organizers I met who were going door to door, talking to complete strangers about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They described connecting with voters who were personally opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but believed the government should never interfere in such a personal matter. One of the most important stories in that election cycle was Tiffany Campbell's. Ms. Campbell, a mother of two, had been delighted to learn she was pregnant with twins. But after discovering a serious medical complication, she and her husband made the heartbreaking decision to have a selective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the life of the healthy twin. At the time, she considered herself a staunch Republican.\par \par \uc2 Sure enough, when South Dakota voters went to the polls, they overwhelmingly voted down the legislation. Two years later, when the issue appeared on the ballot a second time, the result was the same. That was nearly two decades ago, in what had previously been considered one of the most anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states in the country. More recently, in every state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been on the ballot since Roe was overturned -- California, Kansas, Kentucky, Michigan, Montana, Ohio and Vermont -- \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have won. With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot in Ohio, voter turnout in an off-year election reached levels typically reserved for significant statewide races. Again and again, we've seen that when reproductive freedom is on the ballot, Democrats, Republicans and independents turn out in droves to send a clear message that the government has no place interfering in personal decisions about Americans' health and families.\par \par \uc2 A growing number of voters now say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is their top issue in 2024. Voters in 10 states will have the chance to weigh in on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related measures this fall, including key battlegrounds such as Arizona, Florida and Nevada. If Ms. Harris can make a case to voters that reproductive freedom is on the ballot in every state this year, the overwhelming majority of Americans who want to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights restored will have reason to celebrate in November.\par \par \uc2 Cecile Richards is a co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century and a co-founder of Charley, a chatbot that provides information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  options across the country. She served as president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for 12 years.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY CRISTOBAL HERRERA-ULASHKEVICH/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) This article appeared in print on page A23. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240920ek9k0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
